window.GNAMMA_KALAM_PAGES={"title":"Tahdhīb al-Kalām","edition":"Cairo, Maṭbaʿat al-Saʿāda, 1330/1912","sourcePdf":"https://archive.org/download/vahasah/At-taftazaniSadAd-din1912.pdf","status":"First-pass interpretive translation, not a verified complete literal edition.","pages":[{"page":15,"title":"Definition, subject, and knowledge (beginning)","chapter":"I · Introduction","english":"This is the section concerning kalām from the *Tahdhīb*. It contains six chapters, and [divine] success is sought. The first chapter concerns the introduction.\n\nKalām is knowledge of religious doctrines by way of certain proofs. Its subject matter is the **knowable**, insofar as the establishment of these doctrines is connected with it. Knowledge itself admits of no [strict] definition. It can be explained, for instance, as the occurrence of the form of a thing in the intellect; or as a firm, reality-conforming, settled belief; or as a quality by which the object in question becomes manifest to the one in whom that quality subsists—.","notes":[{"title":"The scope of kalām","body":"‘Knowable’ (maʿlūm) is broader than ‘existent’. Sanandajī explains that questions of nonexistence and possibility enter theology insofar as their investigation serves religious doctrines."}]},{"page":16,"title":"Knowledge and rational inquiry","chapter":"I · Introduction","english":"—or as apprehension of a composite or of a universal. These varying formulations draw attention to differences among technical usages of the term. In its real sense, **rational consideration** (*naẓar*) is the soul's movement among intelligible objects, beginning from what is known in pursuit of what is unknown. Its yielding knowledge in a proposition, and even in theological matters, is possible; the status of the knowledge so yielded as immediate or as acquired is discussed. The sophist denies [knowledge]—whether, as in one type, sensory cognition, or, as in another, all cognition; the skeptic suspends judgment between contrary alternatives.\n\nWhether knowledge results from sound consideration by **customary concomitance**, **generation**, or **necessity** is disputed. Consideration aimed at knowing God, exalted is He, is obligatory on the authority of revealed texts and consensus: on our account, because it is a prerequisite for the knowledge that is itself obligatory on these grounds; on the Muʿtazilī account, because it averts the harm feared from punishment. They object: if [the duty of inquiry] obtains only by revealed law, how can someone be required to examine a miracle when no obligation yet exists prior to the establishment of revelation?—.","notes":[]},{"page":17,"title":"Obligation, proof, and beginning of existence","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"The response is that what depends upon revealed law is **knowledge of the obligation**, not the obligation itself. The first duties intended as ends are thus distinguished from the duties of means: what is obligatory in order to secure the [required] knowledge is the inquiry which leads to it.\n\nA **proof** (*dalīl*) is that through consideration of which one may arrive at a judgment. It may be restricted to what yields a conclusive judgment, in contrast with an **indicative sign** (*amāra*). If it depends upon transmission, it is transmitted; otherwise it is rational. A conclusive status can be gained from transmitted evidence through corroborating indications. What reason regards as equally possible in either alternative is established through transmission, whereas whatever the authority of transmission depends upon cannot itself be established except by reason.\n\n**Chapter II: General matters.** The conception of existence is immediate; definitions employing “being,” “actuality,” or “thinghood” are verbal explanations of an already comprehended meaning. Its division into necessary existence and other kinds, together with the possibility of judging existence while remaining uncertain about its specific determination, indicates that existence has a shared intelligible meaning—.","notes":[{"title":"Proof and certainty","body":"Dalīl may refer to an indication generally or, in its narrower usage, to a conclusive proof distinguished from an amāra. Sanandajī emphasizes that rational evidence establishes the very authority on which transmitted proof depends."}]},{"page":18,"title":"Common meaning, existence and quiddity, modes of existence","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"—[a meaning] distinct from the particular modes, and the exhaustive division into existent and nonexistent confirms it. That existence is additional to quiddity **in the intellect** is indicated by the mind's ability to deny it of a quiddity, by the informativeness of predicating existence of it, and by our having to establish that it belongs to the quiddity. The philosophers maintain that the reality of the Necessary is a particular existence subsisting through itself, constitutive of itself rather than something else, and different in its very reality from the existence of the contingent. Thus, unlike the necessary existence, contingent existence can be represented as pertaining to quiddity.\n\nOn our view the existence attributed to existents is predicated with **gradation** (*tashkīk*)—as light is predicated of lights—whereas the assertion that it means exactly the same quiddity in every instance would make its occurrence outside the mind exclusively particular, an untenable conclusion. Rather, it is one meaning in the intellect. Existence is divided, properly, into **extramental and mental** existence; only figuratively is there **verbal and written** existence: the individual human being and the written or spoken designation of that human are not the same thing, just as an individual and the corresponding quiddity are not identical in extramental reality.","notes":[{"title":"Concept and realisation","body":"The immediate conception of existence should not be confused with immediate knowledge that a particular subject exists. Mental addition of existence to quiddity is a thesis about conceptual distinction."}]},{"page":19,"title":"Mental existence; nonexistence and thinghood","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"The extramental existence of a thing is to be distinguished from its existence as a name or form in the mind. The argument for mental existence is that we conceive things which have no external instantiation, and we are able to judge impossible objects affirmatively; we also form universal concepts and true universal judgments. If this cognition consists in the thing's form occurring in the intellect, the point is evident; if not, cognition still requires a relation between knower and known. It is not reducible to a merely verbal connection. Nor does the thinker's thinking of something imply that the thinker is externally qualified by it: a believer can conceive unbelief without thereby becoming an unbeliever. The mind can apprehend contraries without becoming the subject of contraries, and what is impossible outside the mind can be conceived there. The presence thus attributed to what is conceived is a non-fundamental manner of existence sufficient for the knowledge relation.\n\nExistence and thinghood signify **establishment** (*thubūt*); nonexistence signifies **negation** (*nafy*). The nonexistent is neither an independently established thing nor a fixed entity; there is no intermediate state between the nonexistent and the existent. Those who posit such an intermediate category divide and distinguish the two in other ways.","notes":[{"title":"Thinghood and nonexistence","body":"The denial that a nonexistent is a separately established thing concerns ontological status, not the ability of the mind to entertain a nonexistent object."}]},{"page":20,"title":"Refuting an intermediate between existence and nonexistence; quiddity","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"If the non-existent possessed [positive] existence, it would stand in need of something as its cause, or would exist independently, and the distinction from the existent would collapse. If it did not possess existence, it would remain nonexistent. It is thus not a third, self-subsistent kind. The possibility of an object is not a positive thing requiring some existence intermediate between the alternatives; nor does the difference between the **possible** and the **impossible** establish the independent reality of the merely possible. Such discriminations pertain to the intellect. Contradictory predicates cannot both belong to one and the same thing under the same consideration, but statements can differ in their subject and qualification; a meaningful proposition is true insofar as what it signifies corresponds to the case, not merely through the presence of the words themselves. A judgment and the apprehension of what is judged must be distinguished from the independent being of that judgment's object.\n\n**[On quiddity.]** A thing's **quiddity** (*māhiyya*) is what answers the question “What is it?” It may be considered **with a condition** (*bi-sharṭ shayʾ*), in which case it is called the “mixed” [quiddity]; in that form, its instantiation is not disputed—.","notes":[]},{"page":21,"title":"Quiddity under its different considerations","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"—or it can be considered **on condition of nothing further** (*bi-sharṭ lā shayʾ*), in which case it is called “abstract” and is not encountered as such in the intellect, much less in extramental things; or it can be considered **without condition** (*lā bi-sharṭ shayʾ*), which is wider than the mixed consideration and occurs simply as the quiddity itself, not as a constituent of it. If considered as predicable of many, this is the **natural universal**; if considered apart from the attachments and individualizing determinations, these are the **individuals**. The quiddity is sometimes spoken of as matter relative to what is further compounded with it. Regarding [logical] consideration, the simple and composite must be distinguished: some compositions necessarily require parts, while others belong only to an act of consideration or to accidental circumstances. On our position, what is genuinely composed requires constituents, but merely attributive composition does not establish independently existing constituent things. The fact that the contingent requires a cause is immediate.","notes":[]},{"page":22,"title":"Individuation and logical distinctions","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"**[On individuation.]** Members of a single species are distinguished by accidents which may terminate in a matter which fixes their individuality. It has therefore been said that **determination** (*taʿayyun*) and **individuation** (*tashakhkhuṣ*) are that matter, or whatever produces that determination, or a subject's being such that no plurality can share it, or its non-acceptance of such sharing. What is called **negative** may mean the nonexistent, or a privation added to a subject, or something into whose concept nonexistence enters; “positive” means the contrary. What is **real** is what is established in the matter itself without an element of supposition or intellectual estimation; the **considerational** is what lacks such establishment. It is disputed whether individuation is existential or nonexistential, real or merely considerational—.","notes":[]},{"page":23,"title":"Cause of individuation; beginning of modality","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"An individual's individuation may rest on the powerful agent who first brings it into being, or on extramental existence, or on other causes, such as the way a quiddity or an individuating matter is differentiated by its accompanying accidents and the succession of dispositions. In each account one must distinguish the source of the being of an individual from what makes it this individual rather than another.\n\n**[On modality.]** Necessity, impossibility, and possibility are intelligibles which arise from considering a quiddity as simple or composite. Their conception is immediate; definitions that appeal to the necessity of being, the necessity of not being, or the non-necessity of either merely explain one of the expressions in terms of another—.","notes":[]},{"page":24,"title":"Necessary, impossible, possible; dispositional possibility","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"One must distinguish a thing's status **in itself** from its status **through another**. What is necessary in itself is the Necessary Existent, namely God, exalted is He. What is impossible in itself is the intrinsically contradictory, as in the supposition of a second Necessary Existent; and what is possible in itself is neither necessary to exist nor necessary not to exist. Something possible in itself may become necessary through another once the requisite cause is complete. Nonexistence, too, may be necessary through the absence of what is required. Considering whether it is not necessary to be, or not necessary not to be, gives the more general and more restricted usages of “possibility.” One may also consider what is still future: the readiness of matter to receive a form under the fulfilment of conditions is **dispositional possibility** (*imkān istiʿdādī*).","notes":[]},{"page":25,"title":"Causal dependence and choice","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"Some say that everything temporally originating must have a substrate of matter, so that possibility and its dispositional subject accompany every occurrence. But **every possible thing needs an efficient cause**: the impossibility of one of two equivalent alternatives becoming actual without a preferring factor is immediate. This is not the claim that the voluntary agent must always be constrained by a motive; an agent can choose one of two equally available paths, or select one of two desirable alternatives, by an act of will. Suppose it is objected that causation when the effect already exists would be the production of what has already been obtained, whereas before its existence it would require bringing together the two contradictories. The reply is that “need” or causal dependence must be considered as the dependence of the effect's existence or its continued existence upon its cause, not as a temporal interval in which what exists needs to be made to exist anew. Its condition is therefore possibility, understood with reference to the essence and not merely to a past state of nonexistence—.","notes":[]},{"page":26,"title":"Three modalities as rational considerations","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"—[it is not] a genuinely independent third alternative between being and nonbeing. Necessity and impossibility, as well as possibility, are **considerational qualifications**. To call something necessary is to say that its nonbeing cannot obtain; to call it impossible, that its being cannot obtain; to call it possible, that neither alternative is impossible in itself. These must not be hypostasized as externally existent attributes: if their existence were independently necessary, their own modal condition would have to be accounted for, generating the very regress under discussion. When a possible thing exists with its sufficient cause, its nonexistence is excluded, but this does not abolish its possibility in itself nor eliminate the distinction between necessitation by a cause and the causal agent's choice. The three modalities are therefore rational predicates of what is described, not three freestanding objects alongside that which is described.","notes":[]},{"page":27,"title":"Beginning of the section on priority and eternity","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"**[On eternity and origination.]** **Priority or pre-eternity** (*qidam*) signifies not having been preceded by another; this is **essential priority** when what is denied is precedence by another cause, and **temporal priority** when what is denied is precedence by nonexistence. **Origination** (*ḥudūth*) is the contrary. Nothing besides God, exalted is He, is pre-eternal in itself. As for other things, many states are posited by the Muʿtazila, whereas philosophers speak of a large variety of eternals. A pre-eternal effect cannot depend on an elective agent, they argue, because intending the bringing-about of a thing presupposes that thing's prior nonexistence. The reply is that a creative intention need not be temporally earlier than its object; temporal succession cannot be assumed in a question about the ultimate ground of time and being.","notes":[]},{"page":28,"title":"Types of priority; unity and plurality","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"**[Conclusion to priority.]** Prior and posterior may be so by **causal order**, **nature**, **rank**, **time**, **honor**, or **intrinsic determination**; and a distinction is drawn between the case in which one thing has a priority that excludes a counterpart and that in which a prior condition is needed for something posterior. The fact that temporal origination is preceded by nonexistence does not require time itself to be temporally preceded by nonexistence. Nor does the world's contingent character imply the pre-existence of a material substrate as a condition of God's power to create.\n\n**[On unity.]** Unity and plurality are immediately understood. Unity may be **constitutive of** a thing, **accidental to** it, or **relative** to some criterion. Unity in a genus is called *mujānasa* (sameness of genus); in a species, *mumāthala* (sameness of kind); in quantity, equality; in quality, similarity; in property, correspondence; in spatial configuration, parallelism; and in relation, proportion. The account extends on p. 29.","notes":[]},{"page":29,"title":"Unity, otherness, and the distinction between identity and difference","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"The complete identity of two distinct entities is impossible. An attempted proof that appeals to the difference between two essences or two identities is no clearer than the point to be established; yet if there were two existents sharing precisely one existence, their number would require explanation. **Difference** (*ghayriyya*) is reciprocal: a thing cannot be “other” without the other thing of which it is other. Difference is not simply the negation of identity in every possible respect. A thing may be said to be different from another by reference to a whole of which it is a part, or to a property predicated of it. Thus an attribute and its subject, a part and its whole, and the various relations between things need not be collapsed into a single notion of difference. A part, considered with its whole, is not simply that whole, and yet is not an independently unrelated entity either. The formula **“neither identical nor other”** is defended with respect to certain predicates in terms of their **intelligible meanings**, not as an assertion that contradictions are both true under the same consideration.","notes":[]},{"page":30,"title":"Opposition and the kinds of opposed things","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"In addition to coexistence and existence, being **correlative** (*taḍāyuf*) concerns predicates of relation and mutual reference. The related pair mutually calls for the other's conception; their conjunction in the very same respect is not simply the conjunction of two contradictory things. **Contrariety** (*taḍādd*) occurs when two meanings are, by their natures, incapable of belonging together to a single thing from one respect. The philosophers maintain that two contrary things participate in a common genus while differing in their specific forms. If a subject can pass from one to the other, they are contraries in the appropriate sense; if they constitute the presence of a form and its absence from a subject naturally disposed to it, the opposition is **possession and privation** (*malaka wa-ʿadam*); where the alternatives exhaust affirmation and denial, it is **contradiction** (*ījāb wa-salb*). Some accounts restrict contrariety to the case of two extremes within a single proximate genus. Unity and plurality are not always to be treated as reciprocal contraries: in some respects they oppose one another, while in other respects one is understood through the other.","notes":[]},{"page":31,"title":"The cause and its effect","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"**[On cause and effect.]** A **cause** is that upon which something depends. If the cause is intrinsic to the thing, it is either the **form**, through which the thing is actual, or the **matter**, through which it has potentiality. If it is extrinsic, it is either that by which the thing comes to be—the **efficient cause**—or that for whose sake the thing comes to be—the **final cause**. Conditions and instruments are referred to the efficient cause; all the things on which a thing's existence depends are called its **complete cause**.\n\nAccording to us, the being of the effect requires that its cause be complete, because a determinate effect cannot arise without a preponderating determinant; conversely, if all that is required for it has been realized, the effect cannot fail to be. A possible thing does not exist by its own essence; prior to its existence it depends upon what realizes it. The efficient cause may continue to be the ground of its persistence even after its origination, as illustrated by the continuing dependence of a building upon its parts and their arrangement, though a builder may no longer be present. The singleness of an effect as an individual does not in itself establish the singleness of its agent; nor is the singleness of an agent incompatible with the possibility of acting in various respects. The distinction between dependence and independence must therefore be kept distinct from numerical identity and plurality—.","notes":[{"title":"Complete causality","body":"Sanandajī’s analysis differentiates the agent, conditions, instruments and receptive subject. A complete causal nexus must not be confused with a claim that the natural agent is independent of God."}]},{"page":32,"title":"One source and many effects","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"But no one should infer that every complex effect must proceed from several independently complete causes; ultimately, the dependence of the whole leads to the one Necessary Existent. The argument for a principle that from **one, insofar as it is one, only one proceeds**, if understood without qualification, is weak. It is argued that, if two things proceed from one agent, the agent's sourcehood for the first must differ from its sourcehood for the second; yet that difference can lie in a relation of consideration or mode of agency rather than a division of the agent's reality. What the philosophers allege would otherwise entail either composition of the first cause or an infinite regress of aspects. The reply is that the additional aspect need not be a distinct real constituent. Nor must the production of an effect imply that the producer's intrinsic reality becomes multiplied. **","notes":[]},{"page":33,"title":"Physical powers; the impossibility of circular causation","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"The claim that activity and receptivity require two distinct realities is sometimes supported by the observation that the efficient principle is considered under necessity whereas the receptive is considered under possibility. But the argument is answered by denying that such conceptual differences impose, without more, a plurality of independently existing constituents.\n\n**[On bodily faculties.]** According to us the actions of corporeal powers may endure by God's creation. The philosophers hold that they must be finite in intensity, duration, and number: compulsory motion varies according to the receptive body, and natural motion according to the agent. Suppose one of these motions were to begin under the same conditions and continue indefinitely; the comparison between the supposed terminating and nonterminating motion is then used to show a limit. This argument is answered by contesting its presuppositions; even where the influence of the bodily power is granted, the proposed conclusion does not necessarily follow from the magnitude of the body.\n\n**[On circularity.]** Circular dependence (*dawr*) is impossible: it is a thing's depending on what itself depends on that very thing. It would require the thing to precede itself, which is evidently impossible. An ascent without an ultimate cause (*tasalsul*) is likewise impossible in the relevant essential causal order—.","notes":[]},{"page":34,"title":"The argument against an endless regress of dependent causes","chapter":"II · General matters","english":"A series whose members require an independently complete cause cannot be independent as a totality. The whole is not its own cause, nor is it a component of itself, since the whole's cause must be external to the whole. Accordingly an external, independent ground must be affirmed, one that does not itself belong to the series of dependent things; this is the Necessary Existent. Another proof divides the supposed endless series into a complete part and a deficient part and imagines their members aligned: if the elements correspond, a deficient part becomes equal to a greater whole; if they do not, the series terminates—both alternatives contradict the supposition in their respective ways. Nor can the argument be avoided by introducing an infinity of derivative 'aspects' of causehood. For each member to be at once cause and caused would require the real differentiation of the causal and dependent aspects, and with that differentiation the whole would remain dependent. The totality of those that depend is not one that can make itself independent by mere aggregation.","notes":[]},{"page":35,"title":"Concluding note on form and secondary causality; Chapter III begins","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"**[Conclusion.]** Some say that **form** is each individual structure inhering in a receptive subject and unifying it through its own reality or by consideration, while matter is the receptive principle. They give the example of the relation of whiteness to a body and the possibility of form's dependence upon what underlies it. Yet form by itself cannot be an independent, self-sufficient final cause of being. On our account, since **God alone is the producer** (*al-mūjid*), causal efficacy among possible things is an **ordinary nexus of dependence** (*tasabbub ʿādī*), not an autonomous creative power belonging to them.\n\n**Chapter III — Accidents. First section.** Existents that have not been preceded by nonexistence are eternal; others are originated. A thing that has independent self-subsistence is a **substance** (*jawhar*); if it does not so subsist, it is an **accident** (*ʿaraḍ*), which is particular to a substratum—.","notes":[]},{"page":36,"title":"Substance, accident, inherence, and categories","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"—such as life, knowledge, and perception, or not particular to one [subject], as in conjunction and bodily relations. The philosophers say that a substance is that whose existence, when it exists, is not in a subject which sustains it; it is self-subsisting, while an accident is what exists in a subject. They count among the classes of accidents **quantity, quality, place, relation, position, possession, time, acting, and being acted upon**, together with the appropriate category of relative determination. An accident's subsistence in itself, or in more than one bearer without qualification, is impossible; and some accidents necessarily involve a relation to several things, as the unity of a composite structure or the life of an aggregate. It is a disputed question whether every apparent relation is itself a distinct accident. The **accident**, in a case such as proximity, adjacency, or the composition of many things, must not be confounded with the subjects between which it is predicated. Nor can an accident transfer from one subject to another, because its existence in itself is precisely its existence in that subject—.","notes":[]},{"page":37,"title":"Can an accident persist or transfer?","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"—and since individuation is inseparable from that bearer, transferring it would not preserve its very identity. It has nevertheless been argued from the migration of adjacent bodies and from the possible retention of a quality under changes of subject. Disputes over transfer may turn on what is meant by the accident's **inherence**: whether it is relation, restriction to a locus, or the subject's being appropriately characterized.\n\nMany theologians deny that an accident remains numerically the same for two moments, not because the concept of accident formally excludes duration, but because they think an accident's persistence requires another accident of persistence, and so generates a regress, or because the destruction of an accident would require its contrary or the removal of a condition. These arguments are contested; an accident may persist through the continuance of its subject or its conditions, and one must distinguish the passing away of a state from a change in the subject that bears it. The reason for its disappearance cannot simply be a cause that itself presupposes the disappearance. The issue therefore cannot be settled by treating the continued existence of an accident as equivalent to an entirely new accident.","notes":[]},{"page":38,"title":"Quantity and the conceptual problems of time","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"**[On quantity.]** **Quantity** (*kamm*) is an accident that in itself accepts division, in the sense that one can posit a part of it that is other than another part. It is **discrete** if its parts have no common boundary, as in number, and **continuous** if they share such a boundary. The continuous may be stable, like a line, a surface, or a mathematical body; or nonstable, like time. When an added qualification of comparison is taken into account, length, breadth, and depth are distinguished. The philosophers regard numerical quantity as a quantity dependent upon enumeration and spatial magnitude as connected extension. Others treat number as an intellectual consideration, rather than as something separable from its numbered subjects.\n\nTime has been described as a quantity or continuous extension without stable parts. The past and future do not exist as the present does; and the present, if made out of indivisible instants, seems to require first and last instants which are themselves not lengths of time. If one argues that the lack of coexistence among time's parts excludes the existence of time, the same must be tested against our capacity to order successive change. Time is therefore not simply another stable body, nor must its existence require a preexisting temporal duration in which its own creation occurs. The section continues on p. 39.","notes":[]},{"page":39,"title":"Definitions of time and beginning of place","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"The earlier and later are not known by comparing them to another prior and posterior without end; the reality of time is immediately recognized. We therefore divide it into years, months, days, and hours. As to its true definition, some call time the **measure of the motion of the outermost sphere**, but this is disputed because motions are not all equal and a measure of motion might itself require the regularity it is meant to measure. Others call time the measure of a supposed continuously renewed condition, though what changes is never an independently standing body of time. Others say that it is an **imagined continuous renewal**, successive in its parts. The philosophers treat time as a continuous existent concomitant with motion; those who deny this maintain that it is a mode in which change is conceived. One should not infer from an absence of bodies or movement that the conception of before and after itself vanishes.\n\n**[On place.]** Some hold that place (*makān*) is the **inner surface of the containing body** in contact with the outer surface of what is contained.","notes":[]},{"page":40,"title":"Place, extension, and the possibility of void","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"Others define place as **the extension penetrated by the body's dimensions**, as suggested by the equality of a place to the body occupying it, its encompassing every body, and the case of a bird in moving air or a stone in flowing water, where the containing matter changes but the position is treated as determinate. An objection is that two extensions would then coexist in a single locus. It is replied that the status of extension as an independently existing magnitude is itself in dispute. Whether place permits an unoccupied **void** is likewise disputed. Suppose a smooth plate is raised from a level surface: must the intervening space immediately fill, or can there be an interval without body? Or suppose two vessels were separated after their openings had been sealed: does the alleged resistance prove that empty space is impossible, or merely that the contained air or water would need to move? Another difficulty arises from considering a body to traverse the same distance at different speeds in different media; neither the ratio of speeds nor the filling of the intervening space by medium can be assumed without argument.","notes":[]},{"page":41,"title":"Place continued; first classification of quality","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"An imagined void is tested by comparing two bodies moving through an identical region under different resisting conditions: if the motion occurred through an empty region without resistance, their speeds could not be differentiated merely by the medium. The appearance of a void in a lifted vessel or a separated surface is not sufficient to establish that a bodyless interval is actually created. Counterexamples are drawn from the inflation of a vessel, the non-departure of water from a sealed flask, and the behavior of liquids when the surrounding air is prevented from entering.\n\n**[On quality.]** **Quality** (*kayf*) is an accident which by its own character does not admit division and is not a relation. There are four classes according to the usual division. First are the **sensible qualities**, whose tangible principles are **heat, cold, wetness, and dryness**. The cause of heat is disputed: it has been said to originate through bodily contact, motion, or fire—.","notes":[]},{"page":42,"title":"Tangible and visual sensible qualities","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"—and the qualities may also be described in terms of the manner in which matter receives a form. With respect to bodily life, the **instinctive heat** has been called that by which living things subsist; others deny its being one special quality distinct from the familiar kinds of heat. **Repulsion**, meaning the sensible drive in an animal, is classified among sensible tendencies, and it has been said that **natural inclination** includes falling downward or ascending upward: weight and lightness are opposed in that way. The philosophers name these natural movements, while other descriptions distinguish motions forced by external agents from motions proceeding through awareness and choice. Growth in plants, for example, is not an act of deliberative choice even though it has a natural direction.\n\nThe principles of the objects of sight are **colors and lights**. Each includes various species, and the kinds of colors are said to have special names. Whether differences in intensity of color indicate differences in the very essence of color or merely differences in accidental determination is disputed; the status of black and white as kinds is not simply settled by linguistic labels.","notes":[]},{"page":43,"title":"Light, darkness, sight, and hearing","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"Color is distinguished from illumination: **whiteness** is not simply a mixture of light with transparent bodies. Snow, glass, and water show that an appearance can depend on the condition of the receiving medium. Light may be described as intrinsic to a luminous source, as in the sun, or derivative, as in illumination reflected from another object. Some make **light** a positive, separately existing quality; others treat **darkness** as its privation, while others explain visible blackness in terms of an absence of illumination. A luminous object may be a source of illumination without the light consisting in small bodies flowing from it; if such particles were always emitted, reflection and perceptual continuity would require independent explanation.\n\nSome imagine light to be tiny bodies emitted by a luminous object and moving through the intervening region. Others distinguish the illumination of what is seen from the optical condition necessary to see it. Seeing a color in darkness is not therefore simply a matter of having a conceptual image of it: bodily vision depends on suitable conditions for the sensible quality to be present to sight. The conditions of vision and the accounts of light are not exhausted by any one of these physical hypotheses.\n\n**[On hearing.]** Sounds are a sensible quality, and their proximate cause is the motion or disturbance of air following impact or separation. The account continues on p. 44.","notes":[]},{"page":44,"title":"Sound, letters, utterance, and additional sensible classes","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"A sound is produced through a disturbance of the air generated by striking or separating bodies; that this disturbance reaches the ear and that sensation concerns an external object is evidenced by the way sound's apparent direction varies when the listener turns toward or away from it. Sound differs with the proximity and distance of its source and with the passage of moving air. When a physical body is struck, the return or repetition of sound is called an **echo** (*ṣadā*). When sound is subjected to a determination that distinguishes it according to a particular point of articulation or manner of formation, the result is a **letter** (*ḥarf*). A letter may be a **vowel** (*muṣawwit*), such as a short movement or its sustained counterpart, or a **consonant** (*ṣāmit*). A consonant joined to a short vowel is an articulated element; sustained utterance is exemplified by lengthening sounds such as the Arabic *lām* and their combinations. The combination of these elements is called **speech**, **wording**, or **utterance** according to usage. The notion of speech is at times restricted to an intelligible verbal combination, whereas **wording** can mean any combination of articulated elements. The question whether a word is an accident or belongs among the categories of physical qualities depends on which sense of the uttered sound is intended.\n\nThe remaining objects of sensation include the **nine tastes**, the **odors**, and the **four touchable qualities**, together with the ordinary additional classifications of sensible quality. The second class of quality concerns **states and settled dispositions**; the detailed treatment continues on printed page 45.","notes":[]},{"page":45,"title":"Psychic qualities, cognition and mental existence","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"[Some qualities] are psychic, and when they are firmly rooted they are called a *malaka* (settled disposition); while they continue they are described as a *ḥāl* (state). Life belongs to these: it is a principle of the powers of sense and motion, and does not depend [simply] on a balanced mixture, the existence of a particular bodily constitution, or spirit, although it may cease when these are lost and death is its contrary according to some. The term *jamād* (inanimate) may designate its absence. Among the psychic qualities is **apprehension** (*idrāk*): the occurrence, presence, or disclosure of a thing to an intellect [or apprehending subject], either through its reality itself, as is the soul's apprehension of itself, or through its form abstracted from material things or obtained immediately regarding immaterial things and nonexistents. This differs from the subsistence of an accident in a substratum. The subject who apprehends generosity does not thereby become generous, and someone who is generous need not thereby be considering generosity. Whoever rejects mental existence makes cognition a sheer relation or a quality possessing a relation. But how, on that account, can the nonexistent be known? It must be conceded that the nonexistent has a kind of non-independent [mental] existence, by virtue of which it is present to the intellect as known, rather than externally existent.","notes":[]},{"page":46,"title":"Knowledge, assent, and the kinds of apprehension","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"Thus knowledge is what is in the mind, while the known is what is in the external world [in the case of an extramental object]. There are four modes of apprehension: **sensation, imagination, estimation, and intellection** (*iḥsās, takhayyul, tawahhum, taʿaqqul*). The term knowledge is sometimes used for apprehension simply, sometimes for its theoretical aspect, and sometimes for firm assent that conforms to reality and remains settled. Conformity is what excludes composite ignorance, firmness what excludes doubt, and stability what excludes the mere assent of an imitator. As for doubt and estimation, these belong to conception rather than firm assent; the mind's passing away from a form is forgetting if the form has disappeared, or a failure to recall if it remains but is not brought before awareness. Ignorance is either simple ignorance or complex ignorance. Knowledge may be immediate or acquired, and it may be undifferentiated (*ijmālī*) or detailed (*tafṣīlī*). Acquired inquiry can sometimes become immediate knowledge, and conversely an immediately known item may enter acquired reasoning. There is dispute over whether the objects of knowledge are multiplied by relations, and over whether the 'speech of the heart' is other than cognition. There is no dispute here over the use of instruments for the apprehension of particulars.","notes":[]},{"page":47,"title":"Habitual knowledge, desire, will and power","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"The ground of responsibility is the relevant cognitive power. Some items ordinarily called 'necessary knowledge' can be acquired in such a way that they become settled, while the theoretical cognitions are those which require acquisition. **Will** (*irāda*) is one such power: some describe it as an inclination accompanied by awareness of benefit, some as the very belief that the thing is beneficial, and some as a quality that favors one of the two alternatives open to an agent, without thereby reducing it to another conscious preference. The Ashʿarī view is reported to make willing a thing the same as aversion to its contrary, which is disputed. **Power** (*qudra*) is a quality by which an agent is able, according to will, to originate acts of different kinds. The manner of the origination of these acts and the relation between the existence of the power and the act are treated further below.","notes":[]},{"page":48,"title":"The time of power and its relation to action","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"For powers of action, one sort is the animal power; another is celestial, a third vegetal, and a fourth that of the elemental order. The power that is created concomitantly with an act does not, on the [Ashʿarī] argument, precede the act, because accidents do not persist. An objection is that this would require the perpetuation of renewed likenesses, and would rule out the genuine prior possibility of action and therefore responsibility. That is disputed: the power relating to a thing is present with the act, as in the case of the unbeliever's power to believe, whereas the origination of a body or a different creature is not within the agent's capacity. A single power need not attach to two contrary acts. The more precise account distinguishes the quality which is the origin of different acts, occurring through influence or through customary accompaniment, and may exist with the act, before it, and after it, from the conjunction of all the conditions required for this particular act. When those conditions are present, **impotence** (*ʿajz*) is its contrary. Some describe incapacity as absence of power rather than its positive opposite; other explanations will follow.","notes":[]},{"page":49,"title":"Habits, pleasures and bodily conditions","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"Concerning incapacity, it has been regarded as a privation rather than a positive contrary. Others make it a notion shared across [different senses], and the question is disputed. Power is opposed by the createdness of an act, since His acts, according to the theological position being reported, are not an effect of a creature's independently productive power. Whether sleep stands in opposition to power is debated. **Pleasure and pain** are also among the psychic qualities. They may be explained as apprehending what is agreeable or disagreeable precisely as such, although some say that pleasure is a restoration from a state contrary to nature. Each may be sensory or intellectual, the intellectual being more powerful; sensory pain is called suffering. **Health** is a state or a firm disposition from which actions proceed soundly from their subject. **Disease** is the contrary state or the absence of that disposition. Some extend the term to sensible qualities. Finally, the third category of qualities consists of those proper to quantities, such as straightness and curvature.","notes":[]},{"page":50,"title":"Remaining qualities and the definition of motion","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"[The qualities proper to quantities include] flatness, concavity and convexity of surfaces, evenness and oddness in number, and *shape*, that is, the configuration of a body by which it is described as beautiful or ugly. Another is angularity, which concerns the way two lines enclose a surface at their meeting. The fourth group consists of **dispositional qualities** (*kayfiyyāt istiʿdādiyya*): intense aptitude to be affected is called weakness, as in susceptibility to illness; an intense aptitude not to be affected is called strength, as in resistance to illness.\n\n**On 'where' (*ayn*).** This is a body's being in a spatial region. If the presence of a substance is considered with reference to another substance, then, if a third intervenes, there is separation; otherwise there is conjunction. If no such second substance is considered, then, if this presence was preceded by being in another spatial region, it is **motion**; if it was not so preceded, it is **rest**. Motion is thus a being-in-a-region after being in another, while rest is the converse determination.","notes":[]},{"page":51,"title":"Motion and rest: successive occupation","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"Others say that the relevant condition is simply rest. The truth is that **the reality of being in a region is one in all these cases**; the differentiation of motion, rest, conjunction and separation arises from distinct considerations. A single individual could accordingly be said to be moving or resting or connected or separated under different respects. The statement that these determinations are contraries means that their simultaneous realization in one respect is impossible.\n\nMotion, however, can be understood as the successive occurrence of positions or acquisitions of locality in a different region. This is the account of motion as a *thing* that is realized, whereas the other account understands it as the succession of positions without stability. Motion and rest do not require as their condition that the thing remain within one determinate place; if its position is continuously successive we speak of motion, and if it remains we speak of rest. Whether the second instance of occupying a locality, or the sum of two occupations, is motion is subject to hesitation. The proper view is that the innermost parts of a moving thing are moving, while a thing standing in the course of a wind or the running of water may be resting. The precise meaning of **being in a region** is itself disputed.","notes":[]},{"page":52,"title":"Motion in categories","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"[The philosophers define motion as] an emergence from potentiality to actuality **gradually**, either by a continuous transition or in a succession of stages, depending on how the movement and its continuity are conceived. They maintain that there is a continuous feature intermediate between the point of origin and the point of termination, and that its actuality is connected with the movement's uninterrupted course. The accepted divisions include motion **in place**, motion **in position**, motion **in quantity** (increase and diminution), and motion **in quality** (alteration). In place it is a change of locality; in position, a change of the relative orientation of parts; in quantity, an increase or decrease; in quality, alteration.","notes":[]},{"page":53,"title":"Natural, forced and voluntary motion","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"In quality there is alteration, as in becoming black after being white, while the change in a body whose extension becomes smaller or greater concerns quantity. Motion as such may be attributed to the subject itself, as in a vessel's movement, or to its accidents; a thing is called **moved** when motion applies to it, even though another thing is the source of its movement. Motion may be **forced** (*qasrī*), when its mover is external; **voluntary** (*irādī*), when accompanied by purpose and awareness; or **natural** (*ṭabīʿī*), when neither purpose nor awareness is present. Growth, contraction, the movement of the self, and certain elemental motions are distinguished accordingly, with possible dispute as to whether all motions of the soul presuppose purposeful awareness. Natural motion belongs to the elementary constituents through the natures of their forms; some distinguish the generic species, the individual and the contrary directions of motion. A moving process has unity in terms of its mover, its object, its source and its terminus, although its parts can be differentiated through their relations to these. Motion can be contrary in its subject or in its origins and ends, as whitening opposes blackening and ascent opposes descent. It is divisible according to time and its subject; its connectedness is therefore not identical with an indivisible point.","notes":[]},{"page":54,"title":"Speed, continuity and the creation of motions","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"Among the concomitants of motion is the difference between **speed and slowness**, according as its intensity or degree is greater or less. Motion is not a succession of separate rests: that would require the impossibility of motion along a continuous distance, and the absence of a reason for the renewal of one instant after another. Indeed, the account of an arrow's rest at each point would abolish the very movement it was offered to explain. One should not simply say that motion is made up of rests because each divisible path can be divided; nor is the duration of a rest itself a movement. A further difficulty concerns the assumption that, with every motion, the previous motion must have ceased and another have been created. If one uses this to deny every continuous movement, one cannot account for actual arrival, since the arrival would require a new, distinct occupation after each alleged rest without end. Some reply that a cessation is momentary and therefore does not divide time; the counter-objection is that its occurrence at a time does not establish a cessation separating the motions. The discussion ends with an objection concerning the ascent of a heavy stone and the role of a moving cause. [The final line after the rule is a separate note.]","notes":[]},{"page":55,"title":"Rest and relation","chapter":"III · Accidents","english":"**On rest.** When a body moves toward one of two opposed directions, the distance from its starting point measures the difference; when it does not, it is at rest. Rest is the persistence of the relevant relation at the present instant and the persistence of kind at other times. In this sense it is opposed to motion. Some call rest the **absence of motion**, making it a privation; it can be natural, forced, or voluntary, and contrary rests correspond to opposite places, as rest above and rest below.\n\n**On relation (*iḍāfa*).** Relation is the *reciprocal respect* (*nisba munʿakisa*). What is properly called 'relative' belongs to the two correlates; the qualified subject is also called a relative in ordinary usage. The two correlative descriptions sometimes agree and sometimes differ. Their reciprocal being-understood may dispense with an explicit relational particle, or they may require an intermediary expression. The relation may be grounded in a quality found in both terms.","notes":[]},{"page":56,"title":"Relational dependence; transition to substance","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"A relation may be present in one side, or may arise from what is predicated of another; it is displayed in opposites of realization and attribution, existence and nonexistence, intelligibility and extramental existence. Some hold that a relation has a real existence, whereas the majority regard it as an **intellectual consideration**; otherwise an infinite succession of further relations would follow, since the inherence of a relation would require another relation, and so on. They deny that the suppression of every instance entails suppression of the universal as an independently existing thing. The examples of the earth's being below the sky, Zayd's fatherhood and ʿAmr's sonship are invoked to illustrate such reciprocal determinations. The kinds, species and individuals of relation may themselves be qualified relationally, and contrary relations follow from contrary foundations. **'When' (*matā*)** is relation to time, whether the present or some part of it. **Position (*waḍʿ*)** concerns the body's parts being related to one another and to what is outside; **possession (*milk*)** concerns a body carrying or being enveloped by another thing, as by a garment; **acting (*an yafʿal*)** is the occurrence of an effect in another in an uncompleted manner; **being acted upon (*an yanfaʿil*)** is the corresponding undergoing; the remaining predicaments are understood similarly.\n\n**Chapter IV — On substances (*jawāhir*).** Substance is what is divided into body and nonbody; its classes are introduced at the end of the page.","notes":[]},{"page":57,"title":"Body as divisible substance","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"According to the reported philosophers, substance either admits division and is called **body**, or it does not. What is indivisible, if it is a part of body, is a **form** or a **matter** in potential or actual consideration; otherwise it is something extrinsic, such as soul or intellect, according to its connection with bodily operations.\n\n**On body.** For us, body is that which admits division; it can be considered as composed of **indivisible parts**, two or more, and there is dispute about its dimensions. Some say it is composed of a limited number of parts capable of being arranged so as to produce length, breadth and depth; the philosophers define body as that substance in which three intersecting dimensions can be posited at right angles, whether or not those divisions have actually been made. They maintain that the body is compound of a **matter**, capable of division and of receiving forms, and a **form** through which continuous extension is actualized. The mutakallimūn, by contrast, do not grant the same composition. The arguments about indivisible parts and continuous substance now follow.","notes":[]},{"page":58,"title":"Arguments for discrete atomic parts","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"A divisible continuum, if composed of parts, raises the question whether the parts must be finite or infinite. If the mountain is greater than a small stone, then, on an infinite-part account, two infinities would differ in quantity; this is taken as impossible. So also the body's extension, whose parts are supposedly actually infinite, could not be traversed by a moving body, since motion must pass over the intermediate portion before reaching its end. A geometrical argument invokes the point at the end of a line, and the contact between a line and a sphere: if a sphere touches a plane, the contact is at a point, yet a point is indivisible. If a line is made up only of infinitely divisible extensions, the endpoint and the line's contact with itself demand further explanation. A further argument is that points cannot each be larger than one another in a way that would establish a continuous hierarchy of actually existent parts. Thus the defenders of the indivisible part infer that the body must ultimately terminate in such parts.","notes":[]},{"page":59,"title":"The parts of a body; criticism of atomism","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"If three indivisible parts lie in sequence, the middle one must intervene between the ends. The two ends, therefore, cannot meet without the middle; otherwise all three collapse into a single magnitude. A fourth part placed at the meeting of three may be used to argue that the total body grows without possessing a divisible interval. The skeptics object that three indivisible parts cannot constitute real extension, and examples include a smooth ring that turns and a person who looks from one side to another. According to the philosophers, if one denies that continuity and disjunction are accidents, there must be a persisting **substrate** that receives them both. The body is not therefore a heap of indivisibles. Rather, it admits continuity and disjunction; the continuous magnitude receives successive divisions, whereas an underlying matter remains through these changes. Matter is called **hayūlā**, and the form is the extension that gives the continuum its determinate structure. Those who deny separate matter reply that the body itself is what receives the two dispositions, while its continuity and disjunction are accidental conditions. [The final lines introduce another question about the indivisible part.]","notes":[]},{"page":60,"title":"Atomism, continuity and natural kinds","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The dispute concerns whether the atomic part can receive motion and life, whether it can be cut in two, whether it has shape, and whether it may itself be divisible according to imagined dimensions. The advocates of atomism disagree about the number and arrangement of the parts required for body: some assign a configuration analogous to a cube, others to a triangle or square; they agree that the parts cannot themselves contain length, breadth and depth in the manner of a compound. Those who admit discrete substance explain the differences among bodies through accidents that God produces by choice. They argue that the atoms are substantially alike, and that varieties of bodies arise from colors, forms and other accidents. The philosophers, however, say that bodies are of **diverse natural kinds** in virtue of different substantial forms, so that the different operations of water, fire, plants and animals cannot be adequately explained by accidental arrangements alone. The mutakallimūn deny that difference of natural operation proves a difference of substance, since it may be produced by God's will and customary practice. The original text proceeds to the arguments for and against this division.","notes":[]},{"page":61,"title":"Finitude of extension and directions","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The world has finite shape and bounds, and no spatial region or direction can be empty of the accidents and their opposites, such as motion and rest or combination and separation. Its finitude is argued in several ways. First, if a magnitude were extended without limit, a body moving alongside it could never reach alignment with its end, and a straight line drawn parallel to it would generate an impossible inequality of infinite extensions. Second, consider two rays from a common point: as they diverge their separation grows; if that separation were unbounded in actuality, there would be no determinate region in which they meet. Third, divide a greater length into equal parts and place those parts beside a smaller length: if the difference is finite, they must come to an end; if infinite, the smaller and greater would either be equal or yield a contradiction. A further argument asks whether someone standing at the boundary of the world can extend a hand beyond it. The answer distinguishes the impossibility of the required condition from the impossibility of extension itself, and denies that the fact of having a direction makes the relevant region actually existent. Direction is marked by bodily orientation, and the familiar up, down, front, back, right and left exhaust the relevant directions.","notes":[]},{"page":62,"title":"The corporeal world's createdness","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"These are the real and natural directions, involving up and down, and bodies in their substances and attributes. The majority of philosophers regard the **celestial bodies** as eternal except for their changes of position and motion, while elemental bodies are generated and corrupted, although their matter and elemental forms are sometimes held to be eternal. Some claim an eternal substrate from which the four elements—earth, water, air and fire—emerge and into which they return, while others distinguish smoke, vapor, spheres and other bodies. Against the eternity of the world, several arguments are offered. First, a body is inseparable from motion or rest; because neither of these can be beginningless, the body cannot be beginningless. The denial that a single whole can be perpetual through an infinity of successive nonpermanent accidents is supported by the argument from application or matching of infinites. The response that the parts need not coexist does not settle the question of a body's successive motions. Every body is a subject of originated events, and nothing eternal can be the subject of originated events in the sense being defended. The third proof holds that body is the effect of a choosing agent.","notes":[]},{"page":63,"title":"Arguments for creation; the cosmos in philosophy","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The philosophers object that if heavenly motions had a beginning, there would be a time before the beginning and the resulting successive changes would be without an ultimate first. The proof by matching of infinites is again invoked: a whole of indefinitely many motions cannot be made an actual infinite without the difficulties already described. Further, the heavenly sphere and bodies are receptacles of occurrences and thus are not exempt from the argument from origination. A body is also claimed to be the act of a **chooser**: if all that is brought about were eternally produced by an eternal necessitating cause, the entire world would be eternally present. It is replied that the divine will specifies a particular time without requiring a newly arising divine motive; the alleged absurdity that a will should choose one time rather than another is disputed. Arguments from the eternity of matter and time are likewise judged weak.\n\n**On cosmography.** Philosophers classify bodies as simple and compound, the simples as celestial and elemental. What is called the 'outermost' sphere is a simple celestial body above the encompassing spheres, defining the directions. Its apparent unity and motion are discussed in relation to the ordinary celestial spheres. The text then gives a schematic account of their order.","notes":[]},{"page":64,"title":"Celestial spheres and directions","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"**The first celestial sphere** has a determined bodily character; it is said to be placed in position. The philosophers hold that the spheres differ in size and are surrounded one by another, the outermost determining the boundary of directions. They argue that this sphere is spherical, since a non-spherical body would have positions of greater and lesser extension, which would undermine a simple and uninterrupted cosmic motion. A circle of encompassing spheres also allows the distinction of east from west, and the movement of the fixed stars and planets in their ordered paths. The outermost sphere is said to circumscribe the whole. The specific astronomical model is treated as a hypothesis advanced by its proponents, not as a theological axiom. The sphere of the fixed stars is followed by Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, the Sun, Venus, Mercury and the Moon in the conventional enumeration.","notes":[]},{"page":65,"title":"Elements and meteorological phenomena","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The eighth sphere's region is called the **zodiacal belt**, and where its apparent circle intersects the equator are the equinoctial points; the two solstitial points are likewise distinguished. Twelve zodiacal divisions are assigned, each named from a figure, while the details belong to astronomy. On the theological account, the motion of the spheres, like other motion, depends upon the **free creating agent**, not upon a necessary chain of movers; planets and stars move according to the course God establishes. The philosophers hold that beneath the sphere of the Moon lies the sphere of fire, light, hot and dry; then the air, hot and moist; then water, cold and moist; then earth, cold and dry. These elements stand in mutual adjacency and produce transformations called **generation and corruption** (*kawn wa-fasād*). In divine providence part of the earth is made suitable for human and animal life. Fire and the other elements are described as layered; vapor rising from water may reach the intensely cold upper region of the air and condense.","notes":[]},{"page":66,"title":"Clouds, rain, snow, wind and terrestrial change","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"Condensed vapor becomes cloud and then may fall as rain, snow, or hail, depending on the intensity of cold and the conditions of precipitation. Vapor rising together with smoke may be caught in a cloud, and through tearing or rupture of the cloud the resulting sound is **thunder**, while the bright light is called **lightning**, or the thunderbolt when its force reaches a more destructive intensity. Smoke or vapors condensed by cold may move down as wind; different winds arise under diverse conditions. Under intense cold, moist matter in the earth can harden into rock, while the action of sunlight and other circumstances contributes to the generation of stones, minerals and bodies whose composition varies. When vapors and smoky substances accumulate in cavities under the earth, their release may cause earthquakes, violent sounds, and fire. The movement of underground vapors can produce openings from which are revealed wells, springs, or vents.","notes":[]},{"page":67,"title":"Mixture and the distinction of plant, animal and mineral","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"**On mixture.** When finely divided elements meet and act upon one another, the quality of each is modified and a **similar or intermediate quality** arises in the whole. This is called the *mixture* (*mizāj*). If the constituents are of equal power, the mixture is balanced; if one quality predominates, the resultant is proportioned differently. Moderate mixture may be conceived as the condition in which the components yield the appropriate share for an entire kind, species, individual or organ. What counts as moderation accordingly depends on the subject and on the external and internal conditions. The most balanced region of the earth is disputed and often identified by the proponents of the theory with the middle inhabited zone. In the mixture that supports **growth** and nutrition, a living principle is present. When there is nutrition and growth but not sensation or voluntary motion, the result is **plant**; when sensation and voluntary motion are added, it is **animal**. Without those powers the product falls among the **minerals**. Metals and other minerals are distinguished by whether the mixture is altered by stretching, compression and humidity, and by their other dispositions.","notes":[]},{"page":68,"title":"Vegetative faculties and generation","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"Various things are distinguished according as they admit bending, breaking, or melting, or do not; by predominant moisture, dryness, or other qualities. The **plant** shares with the animal the need for natural powers that serve nutrition. One power **attracts** food in the first place; another **retains** it; a third **digests** and another **expels** what is no longer useful. Digestion begins in the stomach and mouth, is continued in the liver, and the products are carried through vessels to the organs. Another power of **growth** draws nourishment into the parts of the body and adds to their dimensions in the appropriate proportion. A **generative** power separates from nourishment what is suitable for the origination of another individual and gives to it the appropriate organizational dispositions. Some attribute the determining figure of the developing body to a further **formative power** (*muṣawwira*). There is disagreement about whether these are really distinct powers or different descriptions of one nature, especially as some of their operations are exceedingly ordered. The opponent demands an account of why they should not be referred to the Creator's operation rather than to independent internal agencies.","notes":[]},{"page":69,"title":"The outer senses","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"with living things' organized forms, plants' remarkable structures, and whether these can be explained by natural powers alone or ultimately require the choosing Creator. **Animal life** has powers that apprehend and powers that move. The apprehensive powers are **external** and **internal**. Of the external senses, **touch** is spread through the body and apprehends heat, cold and the like. **Taste** is a power in the nerve spread over the tongue, by which flavors are apprehended. **Smell** is a power whose instrument lies in the anterior part of the brain and which receives the scents conveyed through the air. **Hearing** is a power situated in the nerves within the ear, by which sounds are apprehended on the arrival of moving air. **Sight** is a power in the two nerves opening into the eyes, by which colors and lights are perceived. The mode of seeing is disputed: some explain it by an impression in the visual faculty, others by a visual ray emerging from the eye. Evidence is advanced for each. The impression of a visible form may remain in the eye for a time after prolonged looking, while the apparent divergence of rays in darkness supplies an argument to those who favor emission. The passage continues with conditions of vision.","notes":[]},{"page":70,"title":"Conditions of sight and the internal senses","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"On our view **vision is created by God**, while the alleged conditions—soundness of the organ, deliberate attention, presence of the visible object, the intervening medium, a determinate direction and the absence of a veil, excessive nearness, remoteness or smallness—are usual conditions, not independently necessitating causes. The claim that seeing requires all of these in every case is rejected. Of the **internal senses**, the **common sense** (*ḥiss mushtarak*) receives the forms brought by the external senses and enables the apprehension of their common objects, as illustrated by the judgment that one and the same thing is both white and sweet, or by the sight of a falling raindrop as a continuous line and a revolving firebrand as a circle. **Imagination** (*khayāl*) retains sensible forms even when the sensation has passed, as shown by recollection. **Estimation** (*wahm*) apprehends particular non-sensible intentions, such as hostility or friendliness; the **memory** (*ḥāfiẓa*) preserves those intentions. The **compositive faculty** (*mutaṣarrifa*) combines and divides sensible forms and intentions; when employed by intellect it is called the thinking faculty (*mufakkira*), and when employed by estimation it is the imaginative composer (*mutakhayyila*). Specific parts of the brain are traditionally assigned to these faculties, but the evidence comes from disturbances of their functions. The final sentence begins the treatment of movement and desire.","notes":[]},{"page":71,"title":"Motive powers and the human soul","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The motive powers include a power that seeks benefits and one that repels harms; the first is called **appetitive** (*shahwiyya*), the second **irascible** (*ghaḍabiyya*). There is also an active faculty that stretches and contracts the muscles and draws the limbs toward, or away from, a direction.\n\n**Discourse concerning immaterial substances.** First is the **soul** (*nafs*), divided in treatment into celestial, intellectual and human souls; by extension the name also designates the principle of the effects of plants and animals, giving rise to vegetal and animal souls. The majority of theologians regard the human soul as a subtle body permeating the bodily constitution, enduring as the body's life is lost and not replaced by the continual alteration of ordinary bodily parts. Philosophers, and some theologians, maintain instead that it is a **substance without spatial extension**, governing the body in its activities. They argue, first, that we judge about the particular and the universal, whereas the body as an organ is only the recipient of specific impressions; second, that the self signified by 'I' persists although bodily parts change; and third, that its relation to different bodies cannot be the same in the way that a body's parts relate to one another. Further arguments concern reflexive awareness and knowledge of indivisible intelligibles. The following page develops these arguments.","notes":[]},{"page":72,"title":"Indivisibility and eternity of the soul","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The soul cannot undergo the division of a body. It apprehends itself, its powers and its impressions, and does not become weaker through an increase of intellectual acts as physical organs do through repeated exertion. None of the properties of bodily faculties accounts for these characteristics. If the intellective faculty inhered in a body, awareness of the body would itself require another bodily representative image, while apprehending the same thing at once in different ways could not be adequately explained by identical corporeal impressions. Souls are said by some to be **alike in essence**, by others to be diverse through their essential properties; their persistence is widely maintained. From their independence of a material substratum some infer their eternity. Others argue that their **origination** is shown by the body's original readiness to receive a soul; if the soul already existed before the body it would have been occupied with another operation, and if souls were eternal and all distinct there would be no account of their finite particularization. Against this, the soul's apparent dependence on the body need not prove that it is destroyed with the body. A dispute arises over whether souls were multiplied before or with their embodiment, and over whether individuation through bodily attachment can make distinct souls of the same species.","notes":[]},{"page":73,"title":"Transmigration and the soul's survival","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The doctrine of **transmigration** (*tanāsukh*) is rejected. If a soul moved among bodies, either it would attach to a second body while the first still had a soul, so that two souls occupied one body, or it would arrive when the organism was already perfectly constituted and become attached to it without a proper account of the requisite preparedness. The perfect tempering of a body is considered to require a corresponding soul, and the supposition of successive souls leads to difficulties. The survival of an intellectual soul after bodily dissolution is not in itself a doctrine of transmigration. What is established by revelation is the persistence of souls and their recompense, not their perpetual passage through animal forms. Claims that intermediate or deficient souls migrate into celestial bodies, imagined forms or animals corresponding to the habits they have acquired are distinguished from what is established by the law. The philosophers agree about the possibility of survival, though they disagree about whether the soul depends continuously upon the First for its subsistence or is intrinsically immortal once produced. The capacity to cease cannot itself be treated as actual dissolution.\n\n**On apprehending particulars.** In our account the soul is the apprehender of particulars through its bodily faculties, including hearing and sight; the philosophers assign sense to the proper sensory powers.","notes":[]},{"page":74,"title":"The soul as perceiver and the grades of intellect","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The faculty of sight is the soul's instrument of vision, and that of hearing its instrument of audition. It is possible for an immaterial subject to apprehend singular things through bodily instruments. The philosophers claim that the abstraction of universals is an act of the intellect, not of an organ, but that does not by itself make the organs autonomous subjects of apprehension. It may be objected that, if all bodily instruments were lost, the soul would cease to have access to particulars: the distinction between the ability to apprehend and the available means of exercising it must therefore be maintained.\n\nThe power of the soul, insofar as it receives perfection through the body, is called **theoretical intellect** (*ʿaql naẓarī*), with four grades. The **material intellect** (*hayūlānī*) is pure preparedness; the **intellect in habitu** (*bi-l-malaka*) has the first principles from which the acquisition of further intelligibles is possible; the **actual intellect** (*bi-l-fiʿl*) can retrieve acquired theoretical knowledge without a fresh acquisition; and the **acquired intellect** (*mustafād*) is the presence of that theoretical knowledge in contemplation. In its operation upon the body it is called **practical intellect** (*ʿaql ʿamalī*), the capacity for deliberation and arranging conduct in livelihood and ultimate felicity. From the first comes theoretical philosophy, the knowledge of things according to what human capacity permits; from the second practical philosophy, directing conduct according to what ought to be. The text proceeds to moral virtue.","notes":[]},{"page":75,"title":"Virtue ethics and the separate intellects","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"The word *philosophy* or wisdom may be applied to knowledge together with conduct, and practical wisdom to the discipline that guides conduct freely. If it concerns the individual's rectification it is **ethics**; if the members of a household, **household governance**; and if those of a city, **political governance**. The foundations of virtuous character consist in moderation of three powers: the appetitive faculty, which yields **temperance** (*ʿiffa*); the irascible faculty, which yields **courage** (*shajāʿa*); and the rational faculty, which yields **wisdom** (*ḥikma*). Together they constitute **justice** (*ʿadāla*). Each has two corresponding extremes: temerity and cowardice stand on either side of courage, while the other virtues also have excesses and deficiencies, such as immoderation and insensibility, and cunning and foolishness.\n\n**Second inquiry: on the intellect.** The philosophers argue that the first of creatures cannot be a body, its substantial matter or form, or an accident, since in each case its production would presuppose an independently acting cause of the same order. Therefore they posit a **separate intellect**, unmixed with matter and complete in act. It does not depend on another bodily subject for its essence or its actions. They argue that the first effect of the First must be simple because of the principle that one simple source yields one simple effect, whereas the diversity of bodies and celestial motions calls for subsequent intelligible multiplicity. The next page gives the emanationist account.","notes":[]},{"page":76,"title":"Philosophical intellects and the religious account of angels","chapter":"IV · Substances","english":"If the motions of the heavens differ, the philosophers posit distinct **intellects and celestial souls**. Their intellects are substances immaterial both in essence and in action; their number is commonly made no fewer than ten, the tenth being the **governor of the sublunary world**. They are eternal according to the philosophers, distinct in species and individual, and intelligent both of themselves and of their causes. They are the principles of the perfection of souls and bodies. From the First proceeds an intellect; through its consideration of its cause's necessity, a soul; and through its consideration of its own possibility, a heavenly body. Such is the philosophical explanation of the successive spheres.\n\nSome claim that the **angels** are these immaterial intellects and celestial souls, that the jinn are incorporeal spirits that control elemental bodies, and that devils are harmful imaginative powers. Other accounts invoke a multitude of spirits of varying orders. According to **our** view, angels are subtle bodies capable of assuming different forms, devoted to obedience and capable of immense actions. The **jinn** are likewise subtle bodies, among whom are obedient and rebellious; devils are particularly associated with disobedience and evil. Their appearing in visible forms on some occasions is not impossible. The text leaves aside further elaboration in order to avoid excessive digression and concludes the treatment of substances.","notes":[]},{"page":77,"title":"The Necessary Existent; beginning of the divine proofs","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"**Chapter V — On the divine matters.** First, concerning the **essence**. Created contingent things need a **Necessary Existent**; things that have originated require what is unoriginated, in order to avert vicious circularity and infinite regress. The revealed Book frequently directs attention to **signs in the horizons and in the souls**, their substances and attributes. It thereby indicates the First Cause in ways readily grasped by the public as well as those who undertake demonstration. Reflection on these signs shows that the Maker of such a world must be supremely sufficient, endowed with the perfections and free from the deficiencies which characterize creatures. The Necessary Existent is, however, not merely the highest being among the contingent beings; **His essence is distinct from all other essences**. His existence must be necessary, and the unqualified possibility or impossibility of His nonexistence is excluded. Being eternal, the Necessary is neither produced nor subject to dissolution.\n\n**On divine transcendence.** He has no parts, is not composite, and is incapable of multiplicity in His essence. He cannot have a partner or an equal. What is next argued is that His indivisibility is inseparable from necessary existence.","notes":[]},{"page":78,"title":"Divine unity and the argument from mutual hindrance","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"Were there parts in the Necessary Existent, He would depend on them; the alleged parts would have to be necessary, thereby multiplying necessary essences, or would be contingent, thereby making the whole contingent. Neither alternative is admissible. If there were two independent divine agents, the two might agree or disagree in what they willed. If they invariably agreed, their independence would still require distinction in the necessity of their essences or in their modes of acting. If they disagreed, then either both conflicting wills would be realized, entailing the conjunction of contraries, or neither would be realized, negating the plenitude of omnipotence, or one alone, requiring the superiority of one and the defeat of the other. That which is unable cannot be God. Nor can the concurrence of two wholly sufficient causes in one simple effect be used to rescue a plurality of gods, since it would no longer be true that either is independently sufficient. The **argument of mutual hindrance** (*burhān al-tamānuʿ*) is therefore understood as precluding the coexistence of independent creators. The text cites the Qur'anic warning that, were there deities in heaven and earth besides Allah, both would be ruined.","notes":[]},{"page":79,"title":"Rejection of corporeality and spatial limitation","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"Different doctrines are mentioned as incompatible with true divine unity: the followers of the dual principles of light and darkness, adherents of various forms of worship of stars or images, and those who affirm a plurality of independent eternal makers. Some attribute acts of evil to the Devil as an independently creative source; others make the souls or celestial spheres self-sufficient producers of their effects. These positions should be distinguished from the theological affirmation of distinct eternal attributes, which does not posit several self-subsisting divine essences. **The Necessary is not a body**, nor an accident inhering in a body. Were He body, He would require determinate spatial extension and composition, unlike the incorporeal necessary being; were He an accident, He would be in need of a substratum. He is not a spatial substance or a thing occupying a region, nor is He confined by direction, conjunction, separation or place. Expressions ascribing to Him a bodily image or direction must be understood in a manner consistent with transcendence, without imagining a corporeal counterpart of the human being. The texts apparently suggesting nearness, elevation, descent or a face do not entail the properties of bodies. The Necessary cannot become contingent or lose His necessary character, nor can what is contingent acquire the essence of necessity.","notes":[]},{"page":80,"title":"Divine attributes are not mere names","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"He is transcendent above body, local motion and alteration. A newly arising attribute cannot be predicated of the divine essence in the manner of bodies, since its beginning would introduce change. Qualifications that are only negative, relational or considered through the changing conditions of creatures are different from the subsistence of a newly created perfection in Him.\n\n**On the positive attributes.** It is true that these attributes are **additional to the essence** in the sense of their meanings, since the meaning of 'knowing' is not identical to the meaning of 'powerful', nor can mere mention of the essence replace every distinct predicate. If God's being knowing were nothing other than His essence without any reality corresponding to knowledge, the affirmation would become uninformative and the attributes would collapse into a list of names. The Muʿtazilites object that distinct real attributes would imply dependence on something other than His essence, multiply eternal beings, and make the divine more like creatures. The reply denies that perfection is dependence, and distinguishes attributes whose existence is inseparable from the necessary divine essence from independent necessary substances. Eternal attributes do not entail a multiplicity of eternal gods. Some objections argue that persistence would require a further attribute of subsistence or an accident inhering in another accident; these are rejected as misconceived applications of corporeal inherence to the divine attributes. The continued discussion concerns divine power and volition.","notes":[]},{"page":81,"title":"Divine power and the objection from temporal acts","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"If the divine power were newly established, its coming into existence would either presuppose a further divine act or involve an actual increase of divine perfection; both alternatives lead to unacceptable consequences. The regularity of celestial motions and the disparity of their effects are offered as indications that an actor must be free to specify different outcomes. Revelation also speaks of power, and the distinction between **the power itself**, **its eternal connection to what is possible**, and **the actual occurrence of a created effect** is essential. The philosophers object that if the connection of the divine power to a new effect is itself new, it requires a cause and a regress ensues; if it is eternal the effect must be eternal. The reply distinguishes the eternal fitness of power to produce from the finite occurrence of its object at a chosen time. To regard the performance of a particular act as necessarily prior to or following its own creation would confuse what it is to act with the conditions for acting. The reality of what is to exist or not exist is known to God, and its specification does not entail a fresh change in the divine essence. Likewise, God's eternal will is connected to the act as occurring in its appointed time, without requiring a new willing. A divine act need not be a production through blind necessity. The arguments of power extend to all possible things, including their beginnings, renewals and final determinations.","notes":[]},{"page":82,"title":"God as the creator of all contingents; divine knowledge","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"**Power** is properly described as the attribute through which the agent is capable of producing its object; **possibility** describes the condition of the object of power. All things that admit origination fall under God's power, notwithstanding disagreements with the philosophers and the Muʿtazilites about the production of human actions. What is produced may be produced directly or through intermediaries, but the whole derives from the First. The **knowledge** of God belongs to the perfections necessarily affirmed. It is supported by the regularity and systematic arrangement of the world and by the divine capacity to act by choice. His knowledge extends to everything that can be known; it is not fragmented, interrupted, or acquired. Some philosophers and others deny that He knows particulars precisely in their changing particularity, arguing that this entails change in the divine knower; this is rejected. Knowing that a thing is to occur, is occurring, or has occurred need not involve alteration in the knower. The change is in the thing and its relations, not in the divine knowledge. This also explains why God's eternal knowledge is not restricted to a general, abstract view of the classes of things, but embraces what actually happens. **Will** is a further attribute, distinct in meaning from knowledge, by which one of the possible alternatives is specified rather than the other.","notes":[]},{"page":83,"title":"Will, life, hearing, sight, and speech","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"The **will** relates to things whose occurrence is possible, specifying when and how they come about; its eternal subsistence does not require the object of willing to be eternal. To affirm a new intention arising in the divine essence would introduce change. The **life** of God is established by the fact that knowledge, power and will are impossible without life. **Hearing and sight** are divine perfections attested by revelation and are not to be understood as bodily organs: God hears what is heard and sees what is seen without being dependent upon an auditory or visual apparatus. Some say the meaning of hearing and sight can be reduced to knowledge; the traditional account affirms them without reducing the testimony of the texts to a merely repetitive formula. Their objects are specified without imposing sensory limitations upon God. On smell, taste and touch, it is not sound to assert divine organs or modes of sensory reception; the texts establish no such corporeal predicates. **Speech** (*kalām*) is an attribute established both by the testimony of revelation and by the prophetic claim to speak on God's behalf. The sign of miracle depends upon God's truthfulness, yet this need not be the speech of bodily articulations. Silence is a privation of utterance, while the divine speech is an eternal perfection. Letters, writing and articulated sounds are signs by which it is indicated, not bodily parts entering the divine essence.","notes":[]},{"page":84,"title":"Eternal speech, verbal expressions and the Qur'an","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"The Muʿtazilites object that **speech** must be composed of ordered letters whose parts follow one another, and that accidents such as sounds cannot endure; on that account God's speech would be created, residing in a body or in another created subject. We reply that **the meaning of speech is what subsists in the speaker**, and it cannot be identified without qualification with audible words. Ordering can apply to the utterances that express it, while the underlying **mental speech** (*kalām nafsī*) is a distinct meaning, neither knowledge nor will, indicated by utterance and writing. The evidence from the common use of *kalām* is acknowledged: people often call the articulated sentence speech, and the term can be used for what is heard and recited. The dispute is not simply over the permissible application of a word. Its strictly auditory form, the wording in a preserved tablet or a written book, and the eternal divine meaning must be distinguished. By ordinary usage the recited Arabic revelation is called **the Qur'an**; in law and worship the language commonly names both what is recited and what it signifies, just as speech in other disciplines can designate the statement or its meaning. The challenge is to explain the relation between the eternal attribute and the temporally ordered words without making the divine essence a composite of temporal letters.","notes":[]},{"page":85,"title":"The speech attribute, *takwīn*, and vision","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"The Ashʿarī account distinguishes an eternal meaning subsisting in the divine essence from the created acoustic signs that disclose it. A further objection concerns the 'abiding of abiding' and the possibility of an infinite series of attributes if persistence itself were counted as another attribute. It is replied that God's eternity does not require a newly arising persistence. Some Māturīdī theologians posit **takwīn** (*bringing-into-being*) as an eternal attribute, distinct in meaning from knowledge, will and power, through which created things come to be. The objection is that if *takwīn* itself were identical to the brought-into-being (*mukawwan*), it must be originated; if it were eternal and necessarily produced its object, then the creation would be eternal. The response denies the identity of bringing-into-being with the creature; the eternal efficacy of the divine act is compatible with the creature's production at its appointed time. The remaining expressions applied to God are interpreted according to the kinds of attributes: many return to the established essential attributes, while expressions such as sitting above, hand, face and eye are not taken as bodily composition, but according to meanings consonant with divine transcendence.\n\n**On the divine states.** The true doctrine is that God can be **seen**: this means that the perceptual relation of vision may occur, as when the moon is seen, without implying an encompassing bodily image, direction, or distance.","notes":[]},{"page":86,"title":"The beatific vision without direction","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"The vision of God will occur for the believers in Paradise. Against this, the Muʿtazilites argue that vision requires direction and spatial location; but these are not necessary constituents of the concept of vision. The meaning of seeing is a distinct cognitive presence which God can create without the ordinary conditions of bodily vision. Nor does the vision of God imply that He is encompassed by sight. Some objections say that God can only be seen if subject to the ordinary light, distance and direction of corporeal objects; the reply insists that those are **customary conditions**, not requirements of what seeing is in itself. If vision is correctly defined as a mode of apprehension whose object is what exists, the distinction between a thing's being visible and the ordinary obstacles that prevent seeing it must be maintained. The object seen need not be corporeal, for corporeality is not in itself what makes an entity apprehensible. On this approach, all existents could in principle be objects of sight, although ordinary experience is restricted to some of them. The uniqueness of the divine existence does not by itself invalidate such a relation. The Qur'anic expression that eyes 'will look toward their Lord', the promise of 'more' for those who do good, and the transmitted reports of vision are invoked for the doctrine.","notes":[]},{"page":87,"title":"Scriptural evidence for vision; rejection of embodiment","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"The transmitted reports and the verses of revelation reinforce the claim that believers will see their Lord. The Qur'anic expressions of looking toward the Lord and seeing Him are taken in their ordinary visual sense, while their interpretation as waiting for a reward is considered inadequate in the relevant contexts. The comparison with observing the moon means clarity and absence of crowding in the act of vision, **not likeness between God and the moon**. The objection from 'sight does not comprehend Him' is answered by distinguishing **seeing** from **comprehensively encompassing** the object. Sight may occur without reaching the whole of what it sees. The verse concerning those veiled from their Lord is offered as evidence that others are not so veiled. The deniers also cite the request of Moses and the response 'You shall not see Me'; the argument is examined in relation to this worldly life and to the different conditions of the Hereafter. There is therefore no necessity to deny the future vision merely because it is not obtained under present bodily conditions. **Conclusion:** God is not a body, does not occupy space, and is not defined by the attributes of created things. Vision adds no such determination to His essence.","notes":[]},{"page":88,"title":"Human acts and acquisition (*kasb*)","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"**On God's acts.** The **originator of a servant's act is God**, while the servant possesses **acquisition** (*kasb*). Acquisition is an added characterization of the servant rather than an independent production of an existent thing: it is the act's relation to the servant in accordance with the specification of one of the two possible alternatives through his power and choice. The Muʿtazilites, by contrast, regard the servant as the effective producer of the act and call this *creating* when understood without qualification. But the existence of everything created ultimately depends upon God's origination; the scripture says that God is the creator of everything, including what people do. If the servant's act depended upon his power in the independent sense asserted by the opponent, the same effect would fall under two powers both determining its origination; or the causal sovereignty of God would be limited. These consequences are rejected. The distinction of acquisition is introduced to avoid **compulsion**, since the act is rightly ascribed to the servant, while its origination is God's. Acts occur under God's knowledge, will and power, without this making every creature an independent creator. Some alleged arguments from the absence of free choice are considered further below.","notes":[{"title":"Acquisition and production","body":"Kasb denotes a human relation to an act whose creation is attributed to God. It must not be translated as an independent productive cause or reduced to a merely verbal attribution."}]},{"page":89,"title":"The Muʿtazilī case for human agency and the reply","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"The Muʿtazilites appeal to what seems immediately evident: everyone distinguishes falling down involuntarily from walking or rising by choice, and recognizes acts arising in conformity with one's motives, purposes and determinations. We certainly distinguish our voluntary acts and can resolve to do or refrain from doing them. These data, however, do not prove that the person is an independent **creator** of the acts. It is sufficient that God's creation of an act is connected with the creature's power, choice and motives as they actually occur; that connection accounts for **acquisition** without making the human a producer apart from God. The opponent also argues that, without a genuinely human act, praise and blame, command and prohibition, reward and punishment would be meaningless. The reply is that such judgments concern the creature's voluntary acquisition, and do not require independent creation. If the servant were an autonomous producer of every aspect of his act, the attributes of created events would escape the universal scope of divine power and knowledge. The argument that God cannot create evil because He would thereby be described as an evildoer confuses creating an act with being the subject who performs or acquires it. So too the assertion that the servant is unable to refrain from an action because it is eternally known mistakes the truth of divine knowledge for a necessitating physical compulsion. The text proceeds to collect the scriptural evidence about creation and agency.","notes":[]},{"page":90,"title":"Divine will, predestination, obligation and human choice","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"The Qur'an attributes acts to servants and also attributes their creation and existence to God. Passages speaking of the reward for what people do, of their responsibility for belief or unbelief, and of their choice to obey or disobey must be read with those declaring divine creation and determination. Such passages cannot be simply opposed one to another, as though one set removed the reality of God's will and the other eliminated human accountability. The decisive qualification is that a servant may be **choosing in the proximate order**, while the existence of the choosing person, the power, and the act depends in the ultimate order upon God's decree. Hence **neither compulsion nor delegation** (*lā jabr wa-lā tafwīḍ*), but a distinction between the two levels. The human is described as voluntary by reason of intention and preference, although the powers by which he acts are contingent. **Decree and predetermination** (*qaḍāʾ wa-qadar*) are discussed without implying that God is commanded by an independently binding law to create each individual deed. A divine command need not be identical with a divine decree to bring about what it commands. The doctrines of divine justice and obligation require further distinctions; misuse of the language of fate to evade responsibility is rejected. The page concludes with traditions in which excessive speculative contention about destiny is censured.","notes":[]},{"page":91,"title":"The scope of divine choice and the objection from evil","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"Some would make God, in every case, constrained to the course that is best and most beneficial for creatures. The assertion that every chosen event must occur by necessity is questioned: the scriptural texts attribute creation and decision to God, whose will is not an external compulsion. The Muʿtazilites argue that a just maker could not will unbelief, disobedience or cruelty, and thus that the wicked deed, as such, cannot be the object of divine will. Yet the fact that a forbidden act occurs is not itself evidence that the command to avoid it is false or that God approves it. **Command and will are distinct**: a thing may be commanded but not brought about, or brought about without being commanded. The distinction between a divine will that specifies existence and an imperative establishing the servant's duty is fundamental. An objection alleges that evil's existence must show that God wills evil as evil. It is answered that creation and moral attribution are not the same, and that the condemnation of what God creates as a trial, consequence or punishment need not be contradictory. The statements 'God wills it' and 'God commands it' have different objects and relations. The text also examines claims that if God commanded faith but knew that unbelief would ensue, His command would be purposeless; the reply appeals to the actual nature of duty and accountability, not merely to predicted success.","notes":[]},{"page":92,"title":"Good and evil; reason and revelation","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"The attribution of good or evil must distinguish what is praiseworthy or blameworthy in ordinary usage from what yields **deserved praise, blame, reward or punishment** before God. [The immediately preceding discussion refers to customary judgments that certain behavior is objectionable.] **On good and evil (*ḥusn wa-qubḥ*).** In the sense of deserving reward or punishment, the author treats them as established by divine revelation; the verse 'We do not punish until We send a messenger' is invoked. If an act inherently deserved blame before any command or prohibition, this would require reason to determine such obligations independently; but reason, on this account, does not impose a law upon God. The Muʿtazilites contend that the beauty of beneficence and the ugliness of aggression are immediately apprehended, and that reason can therefore establish moral obligations. They add that one necessarily prefers truthfulness and saving a drowning person over lying and destroying him, other things equal; and that, if morally recognized truthfulness were not intrinsically obligatory, a liar's claim to prophethood supported by a miracle could not be excluded. The replies distinguish ordinary judgments of usefulness, suitability and praise from the disputed claim of independent religious obligation, and deny that the argument from miracle establishes the larger moral theory. The author maintains that the authoritativeness of the revealed law itself is established through rational proofs about God's truthfulness and prophetic confirmation, not by arbitrary circular appeal to the law.","notes":[]},{"page":93,"title":"Divine justice, obligation and the best possible course","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"There is no disagreement that God does not **impose what is intrinsically impossible** if the expression means what cannot be conceived as the object of any valid act. But they dispute whether a demand can be made of one who is unable under the actual circumstances, and whether that demand is morally blameworthy. If divine command is always limited by the creature's antecedent power, it is claimed, the law would be constrained by the creature's own nature; this is disputed. The assertion that God must always produce the most beneficial act for His servants (*al-aṣlaḥ*) is rejected. Otherwise the existence of disobedience, the possibility of unbelief, and the exercise of trial would be impossible or would require treating every painful experience as itself an immediately apparent benefit. The text distinguishes the case of a command to attain faith and the case of a demand to do something known not to occur. A command can have the purpose of testing and disclosing allegiance, and cannot be judged false merely because its object does not come to pass. It is therefore inadmissible to infer a restriction upon God's power or will from a human preference for one arrangement of events. **A further section** argues that some divine acts are explained through wisdom and purposes evidenced by revelation and consensus, although this does not turn those purposes into independently necessary causes coercing the divine will.","notes":[]},{"page":94,"title":"Divine purposes and guidance","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"If every divine act were necessarily directed to a benefit which God Himself needed, then He would be dependent and imperfect, which is impossible. Some Muʿtazilites say that the benefit belongs exclusively to the creature, especially in the institution of responsibility which makes reward attainable. Yet the principle that every act must have one such purpose does not follow: He is the owner of the whole order, and the arrangement of moral trial may serve forms of wisdom that human reasoning does not immediately enumerate. The claim that perpetual punishment or an apparently slight act followed by grave consequence is intrinsically unjust assumes the very obligation on God which must be established. The text rejects that inference. God may produce acts for wise purposes, including manifestation of justice, distinction of truthful from false, and establishment of order, without thereby being compelled by an external rule of better and worse.\n\n**On guidance and error.** Revelation attributes to God **guidance, misguidance and sealing of hearts**. In our account He creates the state of being guided and the state of error, since He is the Creator. The Muʿtazilites interpret guidance as giving indications, showing the right way and supplying assisting grace, and misguidance as withholding such assistance or judging the wrongdoer to be astray. The terms **favor** (*luṭf*), **success-giving** (*tawfīq*), and **protection** (*ʿiṣma*) need to be distinguished in the ensuing argument.","notes":[]},{"page":95,"title":"Grace, protection, life-span and provision","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"**Protection from sin** has been defined as God's not creating in the servant the sin, or as the creation of a quality by which falling into sin is prevented. The Muʿtazilites distinguish **favor** (*luṭf*) as something that induces the servant toward obedience or brings him nearer to it without necessitating it; favor may be called **assistance**, **success-giving**, and **protection**, while the refusal of assistance is *khidhlān*. The author criticizes attempts to make every such favor obligatorily incumbent upon God: if all possible assistances had to be granted, the existence of human failure would be inexplicable. A further distinction concerns whether favors are efficacious by themselves or in conjunction with the creature's free response.\n\n**On the appointed term (*ajal*).** The term is the time fixed by God's knowledge for the end of an animal's life, and each creature has one determinate term. The person killed dies when the term set for him arrives, although the instrument of killing is a created cause within the ordinary order. The Muʿtazilite view that a murdered person has had his term cut short is rejected: the scriptural and rational account places life and death under divine determination. Apparent descriptions of increasing a lifespan through pious action are explained in the light of divine knowledge and the manner in which decrees and conditional announcements are understood.\n\n**On provision (*rizq*).** Provision is what God conveys to an animal and from which it benefits; each creature receives its provision. Whether unlawful acquisition can count as provision is disputed, as the next page explains.","notes":[]},{"page":96,"title":"Lawful and unlawful provision; the limits of *aṣlaḥ*","chapter":"V · Divine theology","english":"Provision is allotted, and scriptural texts indicate that God guarantees it. Some restrict *rizq* to what is lawful, lest one attribute to God the unlawful as a blessing. The response distinguishes the **physical reception of a thing from its moral permissibility**: the former is God's provision of the means of life, whereas unlawfulness concerns the human act of obtaining or using it contrary to command. The role of the market, prices, and other causes of livelihood is likewise referred ultimately to God while leaving human responsibility intact.\n\n**Conclusion.** The Muʿtazilites have also imposed on God the obligation to realize the greatest benefit and to provide gracious assistance. Their arguments that all omitted favors would entail injustice are rejected: the premise is neither universally established by reason nor entailed by the law. They disagree about whether divine beneficence must be provided in this life or the next, whether its provision depends upon sins, and whether children or animals receive compensation for suffering. The assertion that God must never create the poor unbeliever, or an animal experiencing suffering, is answered by denying the alleged necessary obligation. Divine justice is not identical with being subject to a law outside the divine essence.\n\n**On name and named.** The distinction between a **name**, the **thing named**, and **the act of naming** is now raised.","notes":[]},{"page":97,"title":"Divine names; opening of prophecy","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Some hold that a name is identical with the named, and that *naming* is something distinct from both. If *name* means its signified object, it refers to that object; if it means the written or uttered expression, it differs from the thing named. Thus when 'Zayd' is written, the inscription is not Zayd himself. The dispute is partly verbal. Calling God by a name must be permitted by the divine revelation or supported by a legitimate sense free from any implication of deficiency. Expressions such as 'the Knower' and 'the Merciful' are appropriate, but labels that imply deception, ignorance or dependence are not. The divine names are many, corresponding to the attributes, actions, negations and relations that can truthfully be ascribed to Him. Some names signify the essence in itself; others indicate attributes. The name **Allah** is especially associated with the essence, though the names of God are not restricted to ninety-nine.\n\n**Chapter VI — On transmitted doctrines (*samʿiyyāt*).** **On prophecy.** A **prophet** (*nabī*) is a human being sent by God to convey what is revealed to him. A **messenger** (*rasūl*) is likewise sent, although the term is sometimes restricted to someone bringing a distinct law or book. Prophetic mission is a divine favor containing benefits, complementing the powers of reason and removing the perplexities to which human capacities are subject. The grounds of prophetic authentication are treated next.","notes":[]},{"page":98,"title":"Prophecy, miracles, and rational certainty","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Prophets are known either by **certain proof** or by a kind of **necessary knowledge** produced through their signs. The benefit of prophetic instruction is extensive: it conveys rules and rites whose detailed contents are not discoverable by unaided reason, including the modes of prayer and pilgrimage, the conditions of worship, and many aspects of the Hereafter. A **miracle** (*muʿjiza*) is an extraordinary occurrence associated with a claim to prophetic office and not open to successful counterperformance. Its evidential force is like an affirmation from God that this claimant is truthful; when a claimant says 'I am God's messenger' and a miracle occurs in the relevant context, the event functions as confirmation. An objection is that the act could arise through an unknown natural cause, a special property of some body, secret art, help from a jinn, or another unusual power. The answer appeals to the mode, context and absence of possible successful opposition, together with the customary impossibility that God should confirm a false claimant through just that sign. The universality of the prophet's claim and the failure of those challenged to meet the sign are decisive. The certitude involved is **customary or rational certainty grounded in the full circumstances**, not a bare inference from any strange event. The text begins its proof of Muḥammad's prophethood at the end of the page.","notes":[]},{"page":99,"title":"The Qur'anic miracle and further signs of Muḥammad","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Muḥammad, blessings and peace upon him, is God's messenger, for he claimed prophetic office and produced the greatest of miracles, **the Qur'an**, challenging the eloquent Arabs to produce its equal. Despite their strength in eloquence, their hostility and their incentive to deny him, they could not match it. Their recourse to war in place of successful literary opposition confirms the significance of their failure. The challenge concerns the entire composition, its ordering, sound and discourse, not a single isolated verbal ornament. The answer to the view of *ṣarfa*—that God merely restrained potential rivals from matching the Qur'an—notes that the opponents acknowledged the extraordinary nature of its eloquence and were not reduced to unintelligible silence. The Qur'an also contains information not available from ordinary sources: narratives of former peoples, predictions of future victory, and signs of the fulfillment of revealed promises. Other reported miracles include the splitting of the moon, the moving of a tree, the stones that greeted him, the groaning palm trunk, the complaint of an animal, the increase of water, and the multiplication of provisions. These reports must be assessed according to their transmission and evidence; their combined force is employed as confirmation, rather than treating every individual tale as equally established. Evidence from previous scriptures is also adduced.","notes":[]},{"page":100,"title":"Universality, finality, the Night Journey and prophetic conditions","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Other proofs include the totality of the Prophet's moral excellences and the comprehensive benefits of the law he brought, manifested in its many branches and its influence among diverse peoples. The scarcity of supporters at the beginning, the persistence of the message under opposition, and the spread of the community are noted among the signs. The texts that might seem to imply that his law lasts only 'as long as the heavens and the earth' are explained as a way of indicating a long duration rather than setting a date for its repeal. His mission is **universal**, encompassing all people, and he is **the last prophet**; his law is not abrogated by a subsequent prophetic law. He is the greatest of the prophets, though differences are recorded about the rank of the other messengers. The Qur'an establishes the **Night Journey** to the Farthest Mosque, and the consensus of the second century and transmitted reports are adduced concerning the **Ascension** with the body. Some reports describe the ascent to heaven, to Paradise, to the Throne or to the far boundary of the cosmos; the distinct narratives must be interpreted carefully.\n\n**On the conditions of prophecy.** Prophecy requires integrity, sound understanding, strength of opinion, and freedom from conditions that would alienate people from the message or undermine reliance on it. The preferred view is that prophets are protected from what contradicts the purpose of mission; the exact scope of protection from major and minor sins is discussed in the following pages.","notes":[]},{"page":101,"title":"Protection of prophets and angels","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The protection appropriate to a prophet excludes **deliberate lying in the delivery of revelation**, unbelief, and the kinds of serious wrongdoing that would nullify the confidence of those addressed. The author distinguishes acts committed deliberately from errors of forgetfulness or inadvertence, and reports differences concerning minor sins, their occurrence before or after the prophetic mission, and the conditions under which any such act can be ascribed to a prophet. Grave offenses that bring the punishment of the law or warrant social contempt are incompatible with the prophetic office. Reports of apparent lapses therefore require interpretation in accordance with the purpose of the mission and with their actual evidential status.\n\n**The angels** are protected from disobedience. The text cites scriptural descriptions of their carrying out what they are commanded and not being arrogant toward divine worship. The objections drawn from the story of Iblīs and from the angels' question about Adam are examined. The reply is that Iblīs does not establish sinful conduct among the angels, and that their question did not consist in rejecting God's decree: it can be understood as an inquiry into the wisdom of the creation of one whose descendants might shed blood. The scriptural passages affirming angelic worship and obedience are the primary standard for deciding the matter. The discussion proceeds to the comparative rank of prophets, other human beings and angels.","notes":[]},{"page":102,"title":"The relative excellence of prophets and angels","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The story of Iblīs is not a decisive proof against angelic obedience, since he can be regarded as belonging to the jinn and associated with the angelic company rather than one of the angels in essence. Likewise, the traditions of Hārūt and Mārūt are not sufficient in themselves to establish a contrary doctrine of angelic sinfulness, and reports concerning them require scrutiny. The point of the scriptural narrative is not to make disobedience a natural attribute of the angels.\n\nThere is a well-known disagreement about **the superiority of prophets and angels**. Our scholars consider the elect among humankind, especially the prophets, superior to the angels. Their reasons include the difficulty of remaining obedient amid opposing inclinations and the greater meritorious achievement of the human being whose faculties and desires are brought into order. The Qurʾān's accounts of God's choosing Adam, Noah, Abraham's family and ʿImrān's family, of commanding the angels to prostrate to Adam, and of teaching Adam the names are invoked as evidence of human distinction. Counterarguments emphasize the angels' purity, continual worship, their knowledge of the unseen granted by God, and their nearness to the heavenly realm. The scriptural indications are weighed against one another rather than assumed to settle a purely philosophical ranking at the outset. It is also possible to distinguish the best members of each class from the classes considered generally. In no case is superiority inferred simply from being immaterial or from being human.","notes":[]},{"page":103,"title":"Saintly wonders, magic and the distinction between sainthood and prophecy","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"**Extraordinary events** may occur through the righteous friends of God (*awliyāʾ*) and do not thereby become proofs of their prophethood. The reports of saintly **karāmāt** are to be distinguished from the prophet's **muʿjiza**, because the latter establishes the truth of a claimed mission and is accompanied by circumstances appropriate to that office. Where an event is associated with a false religious claim, the context determines whether it is deception, trial, or another kind of extraordinary occurrence. Saintly wonders may be acknowledged without either abolishing the distinction between prophetic and saintly ranks or allowing someone to dispense with legal obligation because of a claimed spiritual station.\n\n**Magic** is also discussed. The author considers the possibility that some reported effects of magic are real, as well as the fact that some feats are produced by practice, deception, concealment or the exploitation of natural causes. Magic differs from miracle because it can involve learning and rehearsal, and is not intrinsically connected with a veracious claim of divine mission. Scriptural expressions concerning magic and accounts of the evil eye are examined; disagreement over the exact manner in which they operate does not justify classifying every unusual occurrence as a miracle. A legitimate wonder must not be made a reason to doubt the rule of prophecy or excuse disobedience. The highest rank among the saints remains below prophethood, and piety is measured by obedience, not by the sheer occurrence of marvels.","notes":[]},{"page":104,"title":"The possibility of bodily resurrection","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"**On resurrection (*al-maʿād*).** It is possible for what has ceased to exist to be brought back into existence: the thing's **essential possibility** is not abolished by a change of time, and a first existence may even have left the material conditions suitable for a later restoration. The objection that an annihilated thing cannot be indicated or individuated overlooks the difference between the existence of a thing and the possibility of knowing which thing is under discussion. Nor does restoration necessarily imply that the distinction between the first creation and the return is erased. The same individual may be considered before and after an interval in which the earlier existence is absent; temporal difference by itself does not establish the production of another individual.\n\nThe **bodily resurrection** is established by the Qurʾān, the Sunna and the consensus of the community. Consequently the accounts of the Resurrection cannot simply be turned into figurative descriptions of the soul's happiness and misery, as proposed by those who favor a wholly spiritual account of the return. The truth of the body's return does not entail **transmigration** into another being: it is the restoration of the person and the body's essential constituents, not the soul's passing through one unrelated bodily life after another. The discussion of which bodily parts are restored leads into the next page.","notes":[]},{"page":105,"title":"Identity of the resurrected body and the fate of the world","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Resurrection is the return of the same person and not the creation of an indistinguishable substitute. If the original material parts remained, their restoration is straightforward; if some have perished, divine power can restore them. The objection from a cannibal's body—that matter may have belonged to several persons—does not show impossibility. Each person's **original or essential bodily constituents** can be distinguished from material taken into the body through nourishment, and God can preserve or restore what is necessary for the identity of each. The author rejects the requirement that every particle ever incorporated into a body must be restored to the same body at the same instant; that is not what the doctrine claims. Scriptural descriptions of the return from bones and dust give an affirmative basis for resurrection, beyond the mere removal of alleged contradictions.\n\nThere is disagreement about the **annihilation of the world** before the final resurrection, and about the precise duration or order of its transformation. Texts describing the passing away of all things are compared with other descriptions that suggest continuity of certain realities. The **Garden and the Fire** are created and real; the scriptural language that they have been prepared is taken as evidence that they are not merely future figures of speech. Their place and their relation to the present cosmological order are disputed. The philosophical claim that the afterlife must conform to the natural arrangement of the celestial spheres has no authority to overturn the transmitted doctrines, and the existence of a world with different conditions is not intrinsically contradictory.","notes":[]},{"page":106,"title":"Paradise, Hell and the limits of natural philosophy","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The terms of scripture concerning **Paradise and Hell** must be accepted according to their evidential force, although disagreements remain about their location and the character of the regions associated with them. Certain texts describe the Garden as near the highest heaven, while others refer to the Fire in terms that invite different cosmographic interpretations. Their realities do not have to be arranged within the spheres and elemental regions assumed by one school of natural philosophy. Arguments against a world beyond the known celestial order depend on contingent claims about the number of directions, the natural place of bodies, the shape of the universe and the necessity that every element seek a unique location. These claims are not established so decisively that they can rule out another created order.\n\nThe author replies to the premise that the totality of physical reality must fit within the philosophers' hierarchy of spheres and elements. A world created under other conditions may not be subject to exactly the same natural inclinations attributed to earthly bodies. Even if the familiar elements have such inclinations in this world, they do not legislate the limits of divine power. The scriptural announcements of the Garden and the Fire therefore stand, without requiring the theologian to resolve every spatial question about them. The discussion turns from the general reality of the afterlife to particular **events of the grave and Resurrection**.","notes":[]},{"page":107,"title":"Questioning in the grave, punishment and the Resurrection","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"**The questioning of the grave and its punishment** are established by scriptural and transmitted reports, including reports accepted as recurrent or mutually corroborating. There is no contradiction in the Fire being displayed to certain persons before the final Judgment. Objections based on the body's dissolution or the absence of perceptible signs assume that only the ordinary bodily conditions of the present life can make experience possible. God may restore some form of perception to the body or the soul, or create conditions under which questioning and punishment occur even though a living observer cannot see them.\n\nThe text distinguishes the claim that the dead are alive **in the same manner as during earthly life** from the more limited claim that God creates a measure of life sufficient for the prescribed experience. It is not necessary that a corpse be restored in every function for that occurrence. The descriptions of the grave's constriction, the questioning angels, and the rewards and punishments of the intermediate state are accepted according to the evidence of the transmitted sources. The text then introduces the events of the final assembly: **accounting, the terrors of Resurrection, the bridge (*ṣirāṭ*), scales (*mīzān*), the basin (*ḥawḍ*)**, and other matters established by revelation. Their detailed mechanism is not to be rejected merely because it lies outside ordinary experience.","notes":[]},{"page":108,"title":"The scale, the bridge, reward and punishment","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The accounts of Resurrection, the Garden and the Fire are matters disclosed by the truthful Messenger; their details should be accepted on the strength of the proper evidence. The fact that some descriptions exceed familiar analogies gives no reason to turn them all into figures. God can cause **works to be weighed** by giving them determinate forms or by producing the bodies and records through which their weight is displayed. The **bridge over Hell** is likewise possible: the difficulty imagined in the passage of bodies is not an impossibility for divine power. Thus the scale should not automatically be explained away as mere justice, nor the bridge as the law that leads to Paradise, although their moral significance remains intelligible.\n\n**Reward (*thawāb*)** is God's favor; **punishment (*ʿiqāb*)** is His justice. When God promises or threatens, the moral question is whether abandoning a promise or withholding a threatened punishment should be regarded in the same way. The author's account distinguishes the fulfillment of a promise from **clemency** in relation to a threatened punishment. Divine recompense cannot be reduced to an exchange in which human obedience is an independent benefit rendered to God. All acts of worship are less than a proportionate payment for the divine gifts already received. It follows that the extent and character of reward and punishment are to be known from God's revelation, not derived by imposing a commercial equivalence upon the relation between Creator and servant.","notes":[]},{"page":109,"title":"Everlasting destiny and the status of grave sinners","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"There is no disagreement about the lasting felicity of those who enter **Paradise**, nor about the perpetual punishment of those who die in **unbelief**. The case of the believer who dies having committed a **major sin** is disputed. The Khārijites judge major sin to expel the offender from faith and to entail permanent punishment; the Muʿtazilites distinguish the grave sinner from both believer and unbeliever in worldly classification but argue for perpetual punishment if he dies unrepentant. Our position is that the grave sinner does not thereby become an unbeliever and does not necessarily remain in Hell for ever. God's forgiveness and the possibility of intercession qualify any inference from the general scriptural threats.\n\nThe threatened punishment may be remitted, and a person who enters the Fire on account of grave wrongdoing may eventually leave it. It does not follow that God is false to His word: the argument distinguishes an **unconditional promise** from threats whose application is subject to pardon, repentance and other disclosed conditions. The text considers competing explanations concerning the duration of punishment and the status of works. Rational argument cannot require eternal torment for a single finite act merely by declaring it contrary to proportionality; it must examine precisely what the divine promise and threat establish.","notes":[]},{"page":110,"title":"Major sin, cancellation of works and forgiveness","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"There is a disagreement about whether a single **major sin** cancels all previous obedient acts, or whether good and bad deeds counterbalance in some determinate order. One position holds that if the gravity of evil exceeds the good, all good is annulled, and that an equal or opposite preponderance produces a corresponding cancellation. The author denies that such a mechanical accounting can be imposed on the revealed doctrine. Scriptural language about the **nullification of works** (*iḥbāṭ*) may refer to cases of unbelief or to the failure of a particular work through deficient conditions, rather than the annihilation of every otherwise valid deed by one later offense. Verses declaring that God does not cause the good work to go to waste and that deeds as small as an atom will be seen require their own interpretation alongside verses about cancellation.\n\n**Pardon of major sins** is possible for God, and there are transmitted indications that He forgives sins less than associationism for whom He wills. It is not necessary that every grave sin be punished for justice to be preserved. The **intercession** of the Prophet and others, where it is established by evidence, is a further ground for denying the alleged inevitability of eternal punishment for the grave sinner. That subject is examined in the ensuing section.","notes":[]},{"page":111,"title":"Intercession for grave sinners","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The **intercession (*shafāʿa*)** of the Prophet and of those to whom God grants permission is supported by numerous traditions, some of which are explicit about sinners. The Muʿtazilites appeal to scriptural texts condemning unbelief and declaring that certain persons shall have no intercessor. The reply distinguishes a restriction upon **intercession for unbelievers** from an absolute denial of intercession for sinful believers. General negations must be interpreted together with texts affirming intercession by permission; otherwise the evidence is set against itself unnecessarily. Nor does intercession mean that a creature can compel God to overturn an independently necessary sentence. It is rather among the forms of divine mercy authorized by God.\n\nThe reports about intercession include traditions that it is granted to those who have committed **major sins** among the Prophet's community. To restrict it to increasing the rank of those already deserving Paradise does not account for the relevant reports. The argument that one must first repent in order to benefit from intercession would, on the disputed interpretation, render the intercession promised to grave sinners redundant. The author nevertheless requires careful reading of the transmitted reports and their scope. The section then turns to acts of **repentance** and the conditions under which sins are remitted. The enumeration of sins includes unbelief, associationism, forbidden killing and other serious violations, but different degrees of wrongdoing are not conflated.","notes":[]},{"page":112,"title":"Repentance and restitution","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"**Repentance (*tawba*)** in the Law is **remorse for an act of disobedience because it is disobedience**, together with a firm resolution to desist from it in the future. A mere regret at worldly loss or exposure is not repentance in this technical sense. The Muʿtazilites add requirements expressing the offender's disposition, including his sincere acknowledgment of wrongdoing. That repentance is required is established by God's command to turn to Him. The disagreement concerns whether its necessity, and the acceptance of repentance, are established by an independent rational obligation or by revelation and divine mercy.\n\nRepentance is not always completed by an inward disposition alone. Where an act has infringed **another person's rights**, restitution, compensation or seeking pardon may be required: the stolen object must be returned or its value made good; an omitted obligatory prayer or other duty may call for its own remedy; slander, deception and other offenses may require appropriate acts to repair injury. The text distinguishes the **remission of sin** from the fulfillment of a further duty imposed by the original wrong. Some kinds of guilt can be repented of without erasing every temporal consequence. A detailed list includes return of entrusted property, correction of injury, and the obligation to cease the prohibited act. The next discussion considers the remaining duties of the legally responsible believer, including enjoining right and forbidding wrong.","notes":[]},{"page":113,"title":"Enjoining right, and the definition of faith","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"**Commanding what is right and forbidding what is wrong** are obligations grounded in revelation, with qualifications respecting ability and the likelihood of achieving the intended benefit. An attempt to remove a wrong which predictably brings about a worse wrong, or exceeds the agent's proper authority, cannot be declared obligatory without those qualifications. The text distinguishes the cases where the wrong can be stopped by lawful action from cases requiring speech or other forms of counsel. It also discusses when the duty is discharged by some members of the community rather than every individual, and the degree of certainty about the conduct that is required before intervention. Coercion is not an unrestricted power of any private individual.\n\n**On faith (*īmān*).** In ordinary language faith means **assent** (*taṣdīq*). In the revealed religious sense it is assent to the Prophet in what is known to have been brought by him from God. This is the core reality of faith according to the author's preferred account. Some earlier authorities included **spoken acknowledgment** (*iqrār*) in their definition; others included outward works. The author distinguishes what is essential to the inward truth of faith from what is needed for the public application of legal rulings and from what belongs to its perfection. The question whether an act of disobedience destroys faith is consequently not settled merely by stating that good deeds are required by the Law. Proofs from scripture and the received usage of the community are introduced for the distinction between faith and works.","notes":[]},{"page":114,"title":"Assent, acknowledgment and the character of faith","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"To say that **faith is assent** does not mean that every act of apprehension, judgment, information, or acknowledgment without inward acceptance constitutes genuine faith. A person might know a proposition or recognize the messenger's truth while still opposing him. The text discusses the usage of *taṣdīq* and cognate terms, and the implication of the scriptural descriptions of those who knowingly reject what is true. The core meaning is inward **acknowledgment, acceptance and yielding** (*qabūl*, *idhʿān*) to the truth borne by the Messenger. Its object is not merely assent that a proposition exists, but assent to what God has revealed through His Messenger. The Arabic term translated as assent must therefore be understood in its theological context rather than as the minimal logical condition of any affirmative judgment.\n\nThe objection is raised: faith is **commanded**, so it must be **voluntary**, but assent is an inward quality, and how can a quality be voluntarily produced? The response is that being commanded does not require the object to belong to the category of bodily action rather than to a quality or state. One can be obligated to **employ the means** and perform the acts within one's power that lead to its occurrence. The requirement is that the assent be attained through an appropriate voluntary orientation, not that the intellect should produce assent to a known falsehood at will. The treatment also distinguishes faith from bare conjecture, ignorance, doubt and the absence of knowledge.","notes":[]},{"page":115,"title":"Why action is not simply a constituent of faith","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Faith is distinct from **mere knowledge** and from a proposition's being held true by an unconscious or involuntary mental occurrence. Nor is it identical with an arbitrary judgment made by choice against all evidence. The text considers cases in which a person can perceive the truth of a prophet's message yet refuse the inward submission that constitutes the religious act. The use of the word *taṣdīq* does not make faith an unrestricted synonym for every instance of logical assent. Cases of denial, rejection, arrogance, assent or voluntary reception must be distinguished.\n\nNevertheless the **performance of prescribed acts** is not itself an essential part of the definition of faith. The Qurʾān frequently addresses those who **believe** and thereafter commands them to **act**, and in other places coordinates belief and righteous deeds. This linguistic distinction is a principal argument that action can be consequent upon faith without being included in its essential meaning. A person may be obedient or disobedient in degrees while still being rightly described as a believer. The **outward confession** serves as a public sign for legal treatment, while the reality of faith belongs to the heart. The special case of the tongue's confession and public law, and the question how the texts speak of increasing or decreasing faith, continue on the following pages.","notes":[]},{"page":116,"title":"Confession, works and the relation of faith to Islam","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"When early authorities speak of **confession and works** as belonging to faith, they may intend **complete or perfected faith**, including its indispensable practical effects and the means by which society recognizes it, rather than a single essence composed of the inward assent and every commanded act. Otherwise faith would cease whenever any duty was neglected, a conclusion not required by the texts. The author notes several usages in which a person who has committed a grave sin remains within the community of believers, even though his act seriously diminishes his moral state and exposes him to punishment.\n\nThe relation between **faith and Islam** is then considered. The Qurʾān employs both words, sometimes together and sometimes in a way that suggests a difference between inward assent and the outward surrender implied by **Islam**. Some reports identify the two in the sense of the true religion; others distinguish outward submission from the reality of inward belief. These usages need not be contradictory: the meaning varies with what is being contrasted. If *Islam* refers to the full submission to God in the true religion, it includes faith; if it is used for public profession and submission to outward practice, it can be distinguished from inward assent. Likewise the same word *īmān* can be used for its core essence and for a more complete state including its works and fruits.","notes":[]},{"page":117,"title":"Grave sin, denial and the limits of takfīr","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"A person guilty of a **major sin** is, in the author's position, a **believer who has sinned**, not automatically an unbeliever. The Muʿtazilites place such a person in an intermediate status between believer and unbeliever; the Khārijites describe major sin as unbelief. The author rejects both classifications in their generality. Inward faith is not removed by every outward disobedience. Nor can the public legal category of unbelief be inferred merely from a moral failure, however serious, without attention to the nature of the act, the degree of acknowledgment or denial, and the available evidence. Not every sin shows a rejection of the truth of the command.\n\nThe implications for **the relationship between Islam and faith** are again examined through the texts. Reports that the person committing a particular offense is 'not a believer' may indicate the absence of complete faith or of the security and moral qualities belonging to perfect faith; they must be weighed against the general teaching that major sin does not in itself abolish assent. The examples of the Muslim, the believer, the hypocrite and the outwardly professing person also show that the terms function in different practical and theological contexts. The Qurʾān's distinctions among these groups establish the need for care before transferring a condemnation of an action to a categorical judgment on a person's inward state.","notes":[]},{"page":118,"title":"Increase and decrease of faith, and the formula of exception","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Is faith capable of **increase and decrease**? If its essence is the act of assent to what the Messenger brought, that assent, as such, is not divided into additional bodily parts. But it can grow in **strength, firmness and clarity**, and the believer can come to know and distinctly assent to more of the details contained implicitly in the original act. Scripture speaks of increased faith when revelations are recited, and of God increasing believers in faith; these descriptions may concern confirmation, light, certainty, the number of beliefs distinctly apprehended, or the effects and fruits of faith. One should not deny the language of increase and decrease simply because assent has a core definition, nor should one assume that any difference in a believer's degree of perfection destroys the common essence.\n\nA related question is whether one may say **'I am a believer, if God wills'**. Such an exception is objectionable if it expresses doubt about one's present belief. But it may be licit when it refers to the ultimate state of salvation, to perseverance until death, or to the perfection of faith whose final outcome the speaker cannot know. There is therefore a distinction between a present claim of assent and a judgment about one's final standing before God. The author discusses the possibility of uncertainty in the latter without surrendering certainty in the former.","notes":[]},{"page":119,"title":"The faith of the imitator, unbelief and the incapacitated","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"**Faith acquired by imitation (*taqlīd*)** is disputed. The majority consider a person who sincerely assents to the truth to be a believer even without the ability to produce a formal demonstration, since assent is present and no general proof establishes that every believer must personally derive every article of faith from rational proofs. Others object that the person without evidence cannot distinguish a true belief from a merely inherited opinion. The author discusses how ordinary people acquire assent through trustworthy report, the public evidences of the religion, and the established testimony of the community. The obligation of inquiry must be considered without imposing upon every person the technical capacities of a learned disputant. People who are unable to understand a philosophical proof are not necessarily outside the scope of the religion.\n\nThe text considers those **unable to receive or assess the evidence**, including the incapable, and the responsibility of those raised without ordinary access to the message. The cases of ignorance, denial, error and obstinate refusal are not identical. The outward application of religious law may depend on public signs while the judgment of what each person has inwardly accepted belongs to God. The next section distinguishes the forms of unbelief and the conduct that can serve as its outward sign.","notes":[]},{"page":120,"title":"Forms of unbelief, religious identity and wrongdoing","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Not every failure to perform the works of religion is an act of **unbelief (*kufr*)**. The Law may designate certain manifest actions as signs of repudiation, and unbelief in its proper sense consists in rejecting or denying the truth brought by the Messenger. Among its forms are denial of God's oneness, rejection of what is decisively established as religion, attributing to God what contradicts the essentials of faith, and treating revelation itself as false. The categories of the **apostate**, the **hypocrite** and the **zindīq** are distinguished according to the relationship between outward public profession and inward conviction. The denier may conceal his disbelief; one may profess Islam while embracing tenets incompatible with it. Not every disagreement among scholars is equivalent to such denial.\n\nThe author reports the established caution concerning those who **face the qibla** and belong outwardly to the Muslim community: it is unjustified to declare every doctrinal opponent an unbeliever because he differs in an interpretation or commits grave sins. Yet a person who expressly denies what is known as an essential doctrine cannot evade all judgment merely by outwardly using the name Muslim. The text distinguishes unbelief from **grave sin (*fisq*)**, and distinguishes both from lesser error or doctrinal innovation (*bidʿa*). These distinctions have practical consequences for the rites, obligations and public status of persons. The argument now leads to the political-theological question of the imamate.","notes":[]},{"page":121,"title":"The imamate and its public necessity","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"**On the imamate (*imāma*).** The imamate is **general leadership in the affairs of religion and worldly governance**, succeeding the Prophet, peace be upon him, in the management of the community. Establishing it is required for the public order of Muslims. On our account its obligation is known through **revealed law and consensus**: people require a lawful authority to administer justice, enforce the rights prescribed by the Law, settle disputes, and guard the common interests. Some Muʿtazilites seek to prove the obligation through reason by its effectiveness in warding off injury. The Shīʿa regard the appointment of an infallible imam as obligatory upon God because it is a form of gracious assistance by which people are brought closer to obedience. The response is that an alleged universal rational obligation upon God has not been established; if the existence of an infallible guide were invariably necessary in that sense, the absence of a publicly manifest such guide would be difficult to explain.\n\nThe ruler is required to possess **competence, freedom, maturity, justice and sound judgment**, with capacity to maintain order and defend the community. Some conditions, such as knowledge of the Law, courage, power of administration and membership in **Quraysh**, are discussed with reference to transmitted reports. The author argues for the received view concerning Quraysh while examining contrary opinions. Obedience is not unlimited: the requirements of legitimate governance remain bound to God's commands and the protection of the community. The next section considers how the imamate is established and whether the imam must be protected from sin.","notes":[]},{"page":122,"title":"Qualifications, selection, and objections concerning infallibility","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The imamate may be established by **the agreement and designation of those qualified to bind and loose** (*ahl al-ḥall wa-l-ʿaqd*), with a sufficient public pledge and acknowledgment. Some admit appointment by an earlier imam; exceptional circumstances, such as a powerful ruler actually assuming authority, are treated under the demands of protecting the community from disorder. The imam need not be **infallible**, nor the most excellent human being in every respect. A person may legitimately govern although another is more excellent in particular qualities if the choice serves the community and meets the requirements of the office. This is opposed by the Shīʿa, who demand the most excellent, divinely designated and protected imam; some additionally claim that the office depends on a specific transmitted text.\n\nThe author rejects the claim that an imam must be impeccable in the manner of a prophet. The imamate is not a new prophetic office; political responsibility and the preservation of the Sharīʿa are not proof that the ruler receives revelation or has immunity from all error. The community can correct a ruler's mistakes by reference to the established Law. On the other hand, the practical claim that the ruler need not possess infallibility must not be confused with the proposition that any person at all is fit to govern. There must be sufficient authority, knowledge, justice and administrative capacity. The passage then turns to **the legitimacy of the first caliphate**, offering evidence from the acts of the Companions.","notes":[]},{"page":123,"title":"The first caliphate and its evidence","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The author's school holds that the imamate of **Abū Bakr** was established by the recognition and choice of the qualified members of the community. After the Prophet's death, the Companions occupied themselves with settling the matter of leadership, showing that they regarded it as a pressing public duty. The pledge was not treated as dependent on a new prophetic revelation after the Messenger's passing. The author presents the subsequent general acknowledgment as evidence of the legitimacy of the appointment. Shīʿī arguments that ʿAlī had been expressly designated are discussed and denied as decisive. It is argued that, if an explicit and universally authoritative appointment had been announced in a way whose meaning was beyond dispute, its rejection would have required accusing a large number of the most prominent Companions of deliberate disobedience without adequate proof.\n\nThe text appeals to the Prophet's entrusting Abū Bakr with leading prayers and to other reported indications of esteem, although such reports are not all of the same force or meaning. The argument from **consensus** does not require every person to be consulted before the act, but it does require a legally effective agreement of those capable of establishing the office. The Shīʿī interpretation of specific verses and traditions is evaluated against the wider usage of the Arabic terms involved. The chief question is whether they unmistakably confer the juridical office of universal leadership or rather express love, assistance, rank or closeness. The issue continues with a discussion of the reports about ʿAlī.","notes":[]},{"page":124,"title":"Texts concerning ʿAlī and the succession of the first caliphs","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"Among the arguments offered for the explicit designation of **ʿAlī** are the words concerning **wilāya**, and the saying, **'Whose *mawlā* I am, ʿAlī is his *mawlā*.'** The author answers that the word *mawlā* has more than one established sense: protector, ally, beloved, supporter, or one possessing authority in a specified relation. An inference to an unambiguous designation as imam requires additional evidence that the meaning **'universal political ruler after me'** was intended. Similarly, the verses involving the believers' being mutually patrons and helpers may be read within their immediate context rather than as a text of succession. To acknowledge ʿAlī's distinction is not yet to decide every legal question about the caliphate.\n\nThe author also discusses the report of ʿAlī's relationship to the Prophet as Hārūn's was to Mūsā, and other expressions of kinship and virtue. These may prove extraordinary intimacy and distinction while leaving the alleged implication of immediate universal succession contested. He considers the contrary transmitted indications concerning **Abū Bakr**, especially his leading of prayer and reports commending the first Companions. Such proofs, individually and cumulatively, are taken in support of the received ordering. The legitimacy of the caliphate is defended without requiring that its holder be the only distinguished person in the community, and without denying the virtues of the Prophet's family.","notes":[]},{"page":125,"title":"The political character of the caliphate and its historical sequence","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The author resists claims that any failure of perfect moral conduct or complete intellectual excellence **automatically invalidates the political office**. The law requires a real capacity to govern and preserve public religion, not the impossibility of human error. He reports the succession of **Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān and ʿAlī**, and the subsequent change of the political order. The first four caliphs are treated as the paradigmatic early leadership. The subsequent history, in which leadership assumed the features of kingship or dynastic power, is described without equating every historical occurrence with the normative ideal.\n\nThe arguments about the order of excellence are distinct from those establishing the **legal validity of appointment**. One may affirm the particular virtues of individual Companions while holding that the decisions by which they governed were legally effective. The author cites the acknowledged practice of the early community and the priority of consensus in such public questions. He also recognizes that conflicts among the Companions are not appropriately treated as a warrant for indiscriminate denunciation. The historical and sectarian arguments are presented as a defense of the Sunni account against rival theories of designation and infallibility.","notes":[]},{"page":126,"title":"Merits of the Companions and the Prophet's family","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"The author mentions transmitted reports concerning the virtues of **Abū Bakr**, including those expressing the high value of his faith, companionship and service, and traditions in which the Messenger places Abū Bakr and ʿUmar in a distinctive rank. The opposing argument appeals to the extraordinary merits of **ʿAlī**, the Qurʾānic language about love of the Prophet's near relatives, the tradition of the **bird**, the report of fraternity and spiritual kinship, and other narrations about his knowledge and courage. Such material is not simply denied; the dispute concerns whether particular reports conclusively establish political succession or the comparative degree of every person's merit. Their chains and meanings require distinct assessment.\n\nThe virtues of the **Prophet's household** are emphatically recognized, including the rank of Fāṭima, al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn. The author also acknowledges the special status of other eminent Companions and those promised Paradise in the transmitted reports. Excellence in knowledge and God-consciousness is the ultimate measure of honor; the argument over the rank of persons is not an excuse for malice toward the Companions. The general scriptural teaching calls for reverence and restraint in speaking of them, particularly where later controversy has encouraged sweeping accusation. The conclusion develops this restraint through an account of the early civil conflicts.","notes":[]},{"page":127,"title":"The civil conflicts and conclusion of the work","chapter":"VI · Transmitted doctrines","english":"With respect to the disputes among the Companions, the author upholds the superiority of **ʿAlī** in the conflicts and reports the view that the opposition of those who fought him resulted from mistaken judgment, not a simple authorization to declare whole groups unbelievers. **Those who pledged allegiance to Abū Bakr**, those who later opposed ʿAlī under circumstances they understood differently, and those who abstained from participation are not all to be described with an indiscriminate condemnation. The wars of the Camel and Ṣiffīn and the conflict with the Khawārij are understood as legal and political disputes in which error and fault may occur, but the requirements of piety and the historical services of those involved are not forgotten.\n\nThe text rejects the claim that the imamate is identical with an infallible office, and it urges restraint concerning contested interpretations and transmitted accusations. Narratives about later persons of the Prophet's household, the events associated with the end of time, and the appearance of extraordinary signs belong to their respective evidential discussions. The author acknowledges the transmitted expectation of trials and unusual events, without treating every particular report as possessing the same certainty. **The closing admonition** is to preserve the respect owed to the early community and its leading figures while leaving ultimate judgment to God, and to acknowledge the limitations of human knowledge. The treatise concludes with praise of God, the Lord of the worlds.\n\n\n---\n\n\n\nThis draft covers every numbered page but **coverage is not the same as complete sentence-level translation**. The 1912 text's tightly printed Arabic, uncertain graphic readings, in-page editorial footnotes, and embedded objections/replies require direct Arabic transcription and word-level cross-checking. The separate `CRITICAL_PASS_01_PRIMARY_TEXT_ALIGNMENT.md` begins that operation with Arabic–English material for pp. 15–17, 104, and 127. The other pages cannot be called critically established until they pass the same check. No independent manuscript collation has yet been completed.","notes":[]}]};
